window.harnessChunks["rows-9c3eb7b3eb16a131.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T15:50:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Open-Makers/ai-coding-agents-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Coding Agents Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Open-Makers/ai-coding-agents-orchestrator/tree/1c1185c6f323bf3475c3847e38245276852ebc5e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1c1185c6f323bf3475c3847e38245276852ebc5e\">1c1185c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Interpret a coding task, produce/test/edit source files and deliver model-mediated QA/implementation results within the active project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User goals, repository structure, compiler/test diagnostics, code changes, alternative implementation paths and failures under active work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-backed Coder decides concrete implementation/fix content from specifications and current source/test observations; QA chooses test content; PM develops an actionable decomposition. Actual code/test outputs feed later work. - Decision owner: The first-party configured and invoked Go model-backed agents and their separate runner.LLMRunners, assembled by standard main and TaskRunner. External model inference/vendor process logic is a dependency, not assigned independent organizational governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Local workspace/artifact stores, test executor, context source collection, role-specific prompts, optional beads dependency claims, budgets, bounded retries, event bus, source writes and human task gates. - Closure path: User task → PM negotiation/approved spec → model-produced sub-tasks → QA-generated tests / Coder source work and build results → model-driven fixes → audited deliverable/status. - Boundary reachability: cmd/orchestrator/main.go instantiates all roles; TaskRunner.run directly calls executeBeads and implementBead, which route to real model-backed role agents and workspace mutation; no custom downstream operator needs to implement the actor loop for normal use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without model role decisions the program can copy artifacts, enforce test bounds and sequence phases but cannot interpret arbitrary coding goals or choose implementation actions. This is first-party assembled autonomous S1, not evidence of first-party model weights. - Evidence: standard role setup; subtask execution and actual code fixes; first-party role-specific QA inference. - Basis: Standard first-party model/role/workspace feedback paths; no live model-effectiveness test. - Confidence: High for program structure, no empirical success claim. - Caveats: Model/provider is external; the unsupported absence of a provider prevents a live session but not a shipped standard model-backed mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Resolve a concrete inter-S1 incompatibility between QA-authored tests and Coder-authored source, preventing unproductive coder-fix versus test-requirement oscillation in the active project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The coder may repeatedly fail QA-generated tests either because code is wrong or because test assumptions are incorrect; retrying only the coder can cause persistent interference between independent work products. - Distinct S1 units: QAAgent creates actual test files, and CoderAgent produces implementation/fixes; both are separately instantiated role actors and output into the same project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: globalBuildFix observes repeated build/test failure and escalates to QA to discriminate incorrect tests from deficient code. The origin is concrete incompatible code/test artifacts, not mere parallel invocation. - Attenuating coordination relation: QAAgent.VerifyTests reopens production-source/test files via safefile.ReadFile, judges test correctness, and either returns TestsOK (force coder to retry its side) or emits updated test files (repair the QA side). The harness merges changed paths and resumes coder fixes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: UpdatedFiles are written to the worktree and merged into the files passed to the next Coder fix; a TestsOK response records that QA&#39;s tests should stand and Coder work must continue. Retry/QA-verification caps prevent endless cycling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate model-backed QA role owns the distinction between valid and faulty tests, specifically at the cross-unit conflict boundary. Deterministic counters/escalation trigger and file merge enforce that judgment. - Decision owner: Autonomous first-party QAAgent configured by standard main; no external human must personally judge each test-versus-code conflict in the normal path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: defaultMaxCoderAttempts, maxQAVerifyRounds, test reports, separate QA role prompt, source file reader and updated-file parser/writer. - Closure path: QA tests + coder implementation → actual failed build/test loop → QA model reads both sides and chooses retain/fix tests → changed files / judgment return to coder → renewed build/test execution. - Boundary reachability: Standard globalBuildFix is invoked after the implementation of all sub-tasks; it calls installed QA agent&#39;s VerifyTests after bounded failed attempts. This is not a generic queue or hypothetical coordination extension. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The logic explicitly recognizes a disagreement between separate S1-produced artifacts, invokes a function-specific adjudication to identify which side is wrong and feeds the outcome back into their subsequent work. The fixed number of attempts is mere enforcement, not the S2 ownership proof. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the QA model judgment and only retries/fail counts remain; the code no longer decides whether to rewrite tests or require implementation correction. This narrow S2 right is model-owned (A), not inferred from the orchestrator name. - Evidence: QA TDD, Coder and escalation; independent QA source and test verdict. - Basis: Concrete first-party inter-S1 artifact conflict + autonomous resolution + operational return. - Confidence: Medium-high for installed path; no claim QA&#39;s judgment is factually correct. - Caveats: QA authored the tests it evaluates, so this narrow coordination decision is not itself the independent S3 audit witness. Other cross-agent resource contention and infinite loop avoidance are not inferred from it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Govern the currently approved project implementation as a whole by judging multi-reviewer feedback, deciding actionable commitments and directing new corrective sub-tasks to the active work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Aggregate QA, UX and Security defects threaten the deliverable after ordinary coder completion; individual workers cannot decide which whole-project issues are blocking versus deferrable. - Whole-system current view: TaskRunner retains approved spec, task plan/sub-task beads, implementation status, current source files and QA/UX/Security review feedback. The PM arbitration call receives all three reviewer channels in one input and can distinguish current blockers from nice-to-haves. - Current-control decision scope: PM model decides pass versus real fixes, NiceToHave deferral and new remediation SubTasks/review scope; TaskRunner replaces the next iteration&#39;s active tasks with these sub-tasks, creates new beads and reruns implement+review. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A model-backed PM ArbitrateAll deliberates the current global review state and selects the next project-wide commitments; the Go loop limits iteration count and applies task allocations but does not choose which findings warrant fixes. - Decision owner: First-party configured autonomous PM role, invoked in normal green/brown/fix implementation+review pipelines. Human approves initial scope/spec/plan but does not own each automated PM remediation choice in that mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared task/artifact store, beads and dependency tracking, QA/UX/security reports, fix-round counter, maxArbitrateIterations/maxFixIterations, event/status and branch/file outcome recording. - Closure path: Complete work across sub-tasks → code-reality review by QA/UX/Security → PM verdict → fresh sub-tasks/beads → Coder/QA perform remediations → new whole-project review until pass/cap. - Boundary reachability: implementAndReview is called by the shipped run for standard green/brown/fix pipelines. Its pm.ArbitrateAll result is directly consumed to rewrite the task list for the next execution loop. Reduced R&amp;D mode is explicitly excluded from this claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: If PM is removed, TaskRunner takes a permissive &quot;accepting review as pass&quot; fallback; no autonomous whole-project prioritization remains. Model PM owns the substantive current-control judgment, not the deterministic iterator. - Evidence: run modes and user gates; PM remediation feedback loop; autonomous PM arbitration call/parser. - Basis: Positive installed current-plan/review → discretionary new commitments → execution return. - Confidence: Medium-high for structural model-owned intervention, no runtime effectiveness assertion. - Caveats: Whole-system is the one active coding-project/task recursion, not all projects of the OSS maintainer organization. Human approval of task scope is an operational gate, not a separate evidenced S3 parent-owned mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Supply complementary raw-code reality to challenge normal Coder-produced outputs and return actionable independent defects to the current PM control loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Coder might report completion or pass its internal checks while source contains correctness, security or UX defects that routine coder reporting does not reveal. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s claim that implemented project files and a passing ordinary build/test phase are sufficiently correct and safe for acceptance. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder returns implementation files/build outcome through its first-party role and normal TaskRunner status; the project may otherwise be marked implemented after a build loop. - Complementary access path: Independently instantiated Security/QA/UX reviewer roles receive actual filesystem-read project source context assembled by buildCompactSourceContext, plus test-result artifacts. This is different access from accepting the Coder&#39;s completion text. Source context is bounded/truncated; if no source is available, a fallback to raw Coder output weakens independence and is not the positive basis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Dedicated model-backed Security and QA review agents inspect the code and judge Approved versus concrete MustFix; Security does not implement the Coder change in this path. QA may also generate tests elsewhere, but Security&#39;s separate source-reading role anchors the independent code-audit claim. - Decision owner: Separate first-party model-backed reviewer role(s), with dedicated prompts and source read permissions; audit judgments remain autonomous while Go transport and parsing are deterministic. - Closure path: Actual files → independent role review → reviewer MustFix/unparsed concern → qualityReview channels → PM ArbitrateAll → accepted blocking issues become new remediation sub-tasks → coder changes real files and subsequent reviewers recheck. - Who acts on findings: The separately modeled PM agent decides which reviewer defects become blocking new sub-tasks, while Coder implements fixes and reviewers subsequently revisit the changed sources. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: safefile.ReadFile, import/caller context expansion, source/test artifacts, separate role agents and logs, fixed iteration caps and parse-to-feedback mapping. - Independence boundary: Security is created separately from Coder and reads code anew from workspace paths. This is independent with respect to ordinary Coder-produced success claims, though it shares project/operator configuration and model provider infrastructure. It is not a statistically independent outcome study. - Boundary reachability: Standard main.go creates Security and QA roles independently from Coder; normal qualityReview after implementation invokes those agents with actual project file paths and routes their returned results to PM arbitration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove separate reviewer cognition and only source ingestion and deterministic status remain. The auditor&#39;s qualitative distinction among must-fix and acceptable code is model-owned, while PM separately owns corrective prioritization, preserving roles. - Evidence: default separate agents; independent source reader; Security raw source versus coder-output fallback; audit-to-PM corrective return. - Basis: Source-first alternative evidence + dedicated autonomous independent reviewer + distinct S3 return. - Confidence: Medium. Actual raw-source availability and result correctness were not measured; independence is structural and claim-relative. - Caveats: QA test-author reviewing its own tests is not counted as independently auditing itself; the positive witness is Security and distinct reviewer raw-code inspection of Coder-produced material. Mandatory QA in the ordinary production chain would not alone establish S3: the dedicated source-read and PM corrective return are decisive here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No standing environment/future scanning → adaptation-option judgment → return into currently deployed organizational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving dependency vulnerabilities, new coding models, project needs and future tool capabilities could require adaptation, but inspected loops address a currently approved task or update ordinary memory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: PM R&amp;D path can explore user-specified PoCs and memory persists lessons; no autonomous role is shown deciding the orchestrator&#39;s prospective capability policy based on external future-relevant distinctions and returning it to S3 as an adaptation decision. - Decision owner: User for selecting model/configuration and proposing future projects, PM/Coder for the present R&amp;D or implementation task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CollectMemory, finalise-memory hooks, model config/provider fallback, project-context collection and R&amp;D PoC loops. - Closure path: Memory can inform next user task and PM may produce a PoC, but no ongoing future-environment model selects a new capability program and installs it in present operating policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Project-specific innovation, memory storage and re-running task-review do not independently constitute S4 external-prospective strategy. - Evidence: project context and recalled memory; R&amp;D task PoC loop; task completion/memory. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user could explicitly order future-oriented research, but this is a bounded S1 project goal until an operating capability-adaptation loop is shown.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct first-party ultimate organizational identity/policy dispute, legitimate final authority and binding operational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Safety concerns, task scope and human preferences constrain what code can be written, but routine task acceptance is not a constitutional choice for the system&#39;s identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human gates approve/reject task specifications, research and implementation plans; PM R&amp;D can propose ending, and Security may flag must-fix. These are project authorization/content decisions, not an established ultimate identity-policy authority loop. - Decision owner: Human user sets project scope and authorizes work; PM acts within delegated task scope. No S5 owner/path at chosen installed project recursion is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: gateCh, regenerateCh, artifact approvals, security review findings, configured role instructions, model-provider permissions and audit reports. - Closure path: Approval releases task execution or re-planning within the same user task; source does not show identity-level disputes being escalated, decided and converted into durable governing policy for later organizational operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: TaskSpec and human approval are not ultimate policy governance by name, and security must-fix is a current operational safety issue unless it changes final organizational identity rules. - Evidence: task spec negotiation/approval; human plan approval; run gates. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: User governance over a broader software organization can be legitimate, but this project/task&#39;s approval gate has not been shown to adjudicate ultimate identity disputes.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3eb14ad95676a95c0672d7ccaa1777882f752f31/assessments/ai/ai-coding-agents-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3eb14ad95676a95c0672d7ccaa1777882f752f31\">3eb14ad &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:20:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bradygaster/squad\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Squad</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bradygaster/squad/tree/2099faf51c08a912c359209447011b06decf0565\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2099faf51c08a912c359209447011b06decf0565\">2099faf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: specialist agents autonomously implement, test, document, review or otherwise produce project outcomes in their bounded domains. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository/task uncertainty, tool results, implementation choices and domain-specific work state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each spawned specialist chooses local work actions within its charter/task boundary. - Decision owner: model-driven specialist agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent contexts, charters, histories, tools and isolated/background execution modes. - Closure path: agent work changes repository/task state and returns labeled results into the team workflow. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A. - Distinct S1 units: multiple specialist agents executing work in parallel or in dependency chains. - Specific interference / oscillation: parallel agents can have data dependencies, circular dependencies or modify shared systems in ways that create conflicting implementations; the documentation explicitly warns and supplies design-review/dependency handling for these conditions. - Coordination relation: the Coordinator performs dependency analysis, chooses fan-out versus synchronous sequencing, detects circular dependencies and mediates resolution; multi-agent shared-system work can auto-trigger a Design Review so agents surface interfaces/risks/contracts before continuing. - Decisive right / owner: the model-driven Coordinator/Lead chooses the coordination response within the first-party team protocol rather than merely relaying messages. - Supporting mechanisms: routing tables, shared .squad/ state, background/sync execution and concurrency limits. - Closure path: coordination decisions alter later S1 ordering, participation, shared decisions and execution constraints before work proceeds. - Why not generic routing: the positive mapping rests on explicit dependency/cycle/shared-system conflict regulation, not named/domain dispatch alone. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P). - Autonomous mode function: regulate live whole-team commitments, assignment, concurrency, intervention and exception handling. - Whole-system current view: Coordinator/Ralph consume team state, available issues, recent decisions, work status and agent capabilities. - Autonomous decisive right / owner: Coordinator/Ralph-backed agent logic selects work, chooses which members execute it, decides parallel/sync structure, monitors execution and applies/recommends intervention; reviewer rejection can force reassignment rather than letting an S1 self-repair indefinitely. - Supporting mechanisms: routing manifests, orchestration logs, watch polling, status/health surfaces and tiered recovery enforcement. - Autonomous closure: decisions change assignment/execution/recovery state and subsequent team operation. - Parent mode: when current control exceeds autonomous authority, the first-party protocol pauses/routes to a human member or escalates to the user. If all capable agents are reviewer-locked, the Coordinator offers parent choices such as manual fix, unlock with guidance or close; the returned human decision changes subsequent current operation. - Parent owner: legitimate human team member/user at the project recursion. - Why A(P): autonomous current-control closure and a distinct operationally closed human-parent mode are both first-party supported; they are alternative ownership modes, not simultaneous dual control. - Basis /\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A. - Claim being audited: that an S1 agent&#39;s submitted implementation/work is acceptable for correctness, quality, architecture/security or test criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing agent returns its result/history through the Coordinator. - Complementary access path: a separate Lead/Tester reviewer inspects the work under reviewer authority rather than relying on the author&#39;s own success claim. - Independence boundary: on rejection, the original author is locked out from self-revision for that task; the Coordinator must reassign work to another agent or escalate, preventing the audited S1 from owning both judgment and corrective disposition. - Decisive feedback right / owner: the reviewer agent approves or rejects within its stated scope. - Closure path: approval permits progression; rejection records the finding, locks the author and routes correction to a different actor or parent authority. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Retrospectives, personal histories, shared decisions and reusable skills improve organizational memory and can change later behaviour, but the reviewed evidence is primarily internal/project-experience learning. It does not establish a distinct externally and prospectively oriented environmental-intelligence function that models changing future conditions, develops adaptation options and closes them into present capability through S3. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: composition and role identity of the team, durable project directives/conventions and final resolution of decisions that the organization may not override autonomously. - First-party parent path: during initialization Squad proposes a roster, but the human confirms/customizes it before materialization; later the user may add/remove/redefine members and establish durable “always/never” directives. - Ultimate authority: the documentation names the user as the final arbiter who can override any decision. - Return-to-operation path: parent roster/charter changes are materialized into .squad/team.md/agent charters/routing, while durable directives enter decisions.md; subsequent agents read the current team/decision state before working, so the returned parent decision governs later operation. - Why not autonomous S5: Coordinator/Scribe may propose or propagate policy-like material, but the reviewed first-party boundary does not give them legitimate ultimate authority over team identity or final policy against the user. - Why P rather than generic HITL: the evidence concerns organization identity/durable policy and includes a reconstructable parent decision → persisted rule/team definition → subsequent-operation closure. - Basis /\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96a771605cae253b024233012a564fafde18c029/assessments/squad.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96a771605cae253b024233012a564fafde18c029\">96a7716 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T20:13:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oh-my-pi</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/tree/dbf3afad4894bde827d90f965e77b3fe1c5a95e5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbf3afad4894bde827d90f965e77b3fe1c5a95e5\">dbf3afa &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the primary and delegated coding agents autonomously perform useful repository/tool work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the first-party task surface exposes a model-selected isolated-worktree mode specifically addressing sibling write/merge interference; the parent agent chooses which spawned work is isolated while the runtime enforces separation and merge/patch handling. Peer Hub messaging additionally returns coordination feedback between distinct S1s, but generic messaging alone is not the basis for the positive state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the autonomous primary agent receives a current control surface through hub: it can observe its active peers/background commitments, cancel owned jobs, steer/revive peers through messaging, and start/stop/restart shared supervised processes. These interventions alter current capacity/commitments while deterministic registries/job managers/brokers enforce the decisions. Separately, the human Agent Hub exposes the current subagent roster and lets the parent steer, revive and kill members, establishing a distinct parent-governed S3 mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Advisor is a distinct reviewer actor with its own model/context/ToolSession and default independent workspace-inspection tools. It can challenge the primary trajectory and return findings that change subsequent execution through steering/blocker feedback. This supplies materially complementary access rather than merely re-reading the primary&#39;s final report.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: Advisor review, memory, model fallback and lifecycle maintenance concern present operation/quality; no separate prospective environment-facing adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: approval modes, user settings and human session control define operational permissions and configuration. No first-party identity/ultimate-policy matter with legitimate S5 closure was established, so generic user authority is not published as S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c/assessments/oh-my-pi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c\">3c76cda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:17:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/the-open-engine/zeroshot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">zeroshot</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/the-open-engine/zeroshot/tree/4c2ed7d5cf3dce968ef7634a2bb49f4382226637\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4c2ed7d5cf3dce968ef7634a2bb49f4382226637\">4c2ed7d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement user-requested code changes or run research experiments with model-directed tool/file actions and return artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: arbitrary codebase tasks, repository state, model/tool feedback, tests and experiment outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: instantiated worker/researcher agent selects substantive coding or experiment actions; zeroshot graph invokes and retains the role under a specific runtime binding. - Decision owner: the first-party configured graph node&#39;s autonomous agent role through a supported harness adapter; external harness internals retain lower-level execution but are not separately scored. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/harness binding, graph transitions, workspace/capsule, runner, provider session, SQLite ledger and bounded retries. - Closure path: task input → worker/researcher model/tool action → workspace artifact/experiment evidence → result returned to graph and, after review, revised worker action or accepted output. - Boundary reachability: zeroshot run --template software-change and auto-research are built-in supported native CLI/Docker graph modes with first-party runner and first-party role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph plumbing cannot implement semantic code changes or choose experiments without a model-driven worker; the autonomous operational actor is necessary. - Evidence: README; nativev2templates.rs; autoresearch.rs. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between independently active run/postmortem cells that share a scoped lesson store and might overwrite contradictory or stale guidance. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing runs and their postmortem maintenance/discovery agents, each producing prospective repository/graph advice for the same shared memory scope. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent updates to a lesson from stale snapshots, resurrecting a retired lesson, incompatible parallel advice replacing a newer record and duplicated overlapping recommendations. - Attenuating coordination relation: each specialist returns keep/adapt/retire/defer for frozen lesson IDs; host requires expected revisions and atomic publication, rejects conflicting writes, reloads the latest lesson snapshot, then re-invokes model reconciliation rather than accepting last-writer-wins. Same-scope overlap is consolidated; unrelated scopes cannot rewrite each other. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a publication conflict suspends the stale mutation and returns refreshed data to maintenance agents; only successfully reconciled newer cards become injectable guidance into subsequent model/tool sessions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the evidence identifies actual competing independent agent/run modifications of one shared knowledge resource and a conflict-specific reject/reconcile/return path, not merely a datastore or graph edge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cross-run retained-guidance collisions and stale-write interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to reconcile a stale/overlapping scoped lesson so concurrent-run advice can coexist or supersede safely. - Decision owner: first-party optimistic-concurrency and scoped specialist reconciliation constructor; autonomous maintenance judgments are available but the general inter-run arbitration mode remains scoped/composed, not a universal S2 organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned SQLite transactions, tombstones, stable IDs, scoped snapshots, conflict detection, specialist result schema and refresh/retry budget. - Closure path: concurrent postmortem decisions → CAS/version conflict → refreshed scope + specialist reconsideration → atomic accepted lesson revisions → future run retrieval or explicit failed reconciliation. - Boundary reachability: this is the supported local/Docker Active Memory path described in guide, implemented in postmortem.rs and store.rs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party mechanism explicitly constructs a recoverable S2-specific path; it does not establish broadly agent-owned coordination of every simultaneous run, so the conservative publication is C. - Evidence: Active Memory; postmortem.rs; store.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: conflict witness is shared retained lessons across runs, not all inter-agent workflow interference. Worker/reviewer fanout and graph scheduling alone are not used as S2 proof.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: manage the current auto-research program and its experiment/review/correction choices across distinct scouting and research S1 units; not all unrelated zeroshot installations. - Whole-system current view: one auto-research run&#39;s three scout proposals, current experiment/report, reviewer verdict and feedback, retained candidate and bounded iteration state. - Current-control decision scope: select which current approach/experiment to execute, revise work from review evidence, retain accepted result or stop a blocked/inconclusive round. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing scout directions, experimental failure, reviewer rejection and incomplete current research commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven researcher chooses experiment and revision priorities from scouts and review, while native graph enforces maximum iterations and terminal decisions. - Decision owner: first-party instantiated researcher Agent in the built-in auto-research graph; graph scheduler supplies deterministic execution constraints, and fresh reviewer provides challenge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Par scout fan-in, researcher continuation session, bounded inner review loop, current state/feedback fields, SQLite run ledger and optional push gate. - Closure path: three model scout proposals → researcher decision/experiment → fresh reviewer evidence → same researcher revises or accepts → next round&#39;s current plan/retained result. - Boundary reachability: auto-research native template with shipped researcher instructions is built-in, not a user-authored hypothetical graph. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model researcher owns current experiment selection and corrective judgment; removing it leaves fixed fan-in/limits but not the substantive cross-scout control choice. - Evidence: README auto-research; autoresearch.rs; researcher.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: S3=A is specific to the built-in multi-agent research-program mode. In software-change, fixed graph control and reviewer gates alone are not credited as an independent model-driven S3 controller.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: test the implementer&#39;s outcome through fresh independent acceptance/code reviewers, then force rework on rejection before delivery or successful closure. - Claim being audited: worker&#39;s code meets requested acceptance criteria and code-quality/correctness expectations, not merely that the worker completed a turn. - Ordinary reporting path: worker writes/edit files, returns output and claims completed implementation in its retained session. - Complementary access path: separate acceptance and code reviewer nodes run in parallel against the shared workspace, each with its own fresh model session and rubric; their structured verdict/diagnostic inputs are not copied from the worker&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: both reviewers start fresh on every pass while implementation/repair uses the same worker session; the native graph gives reviewer roles separate identities and returns both findings. - Who acts on findings: enforced graph routes rejected reviewer evidence back to original worker; the next pass repeats both independent reviewers. Delivery/CI conflicts may also return through controlled repair and re-review. Acceptance cannot bypass guards with a prose-only assertion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect self-reported success, acceptance gaps, code defects, regression and unreviewed delivery changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately model-driven reviewer decides accepted/rejected criteria and can prevent progression, triggering another worker repair round. - Decision owner: first-party instantiated independent reviewer/verifier agents for the standard software-change mode; trusted native graph gates/runner enforce their verdicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed graph signals, Par reviewer join, guard routing, bounded global loop budget, retained worker session, fresh reviewer sessions, run ledger and CI/delivery gates. - Closure path: worker code → fresh acceptance+code review → accepted or rejection diagnostics → worker repair → new review pass → accepted artifact/delivery or bounded honest failure. - Boundary reachability: the default software-change template and review/repair continuity tests demonstrate a standard operational path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewers independently choose substantive feedback; graph machinery supplies independent session enforcement and corrective return, not semantic acceptance decisions itself. - Evidence: README; nativev2templates.rs; continuity.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: independence is of decision sessions/evidence roles, not guaranteed model-provider diversity or exhaustive correctness. User-specified graph/model settings can alter actual review coverage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: discover and reconcile costly recurring errors and outdated procedural knowledge so later graph/repository agent work is better adapted to changed evidence. - External distinction: real repository files and tool receipts, repeated costly failures/rejected review loops, current scope content and changed advice provenance, rather than the just-finished worker&#39;s optimistic summary. - Future / prospective distinction: determine which recurring or newly learned guidance will remain useful across subsequent runs, adapt stale lessons to current source and retire obsoleted/conflicting advice. - Adaptation option generated: graph and repository discovery specialists propose new procedural cards; separate maintenance specialists keep/adapt/retire/defer all supplied lessons, consolidate overlaps and identify stable recommendations. - Path back into current capability / S3: versioned accepted cards are stored per graph/node/repository and injected through native pre-tool hooks or graph instructions in future sessions, changing model choices in later S1/S3 work without rewriting the authored graph or policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repetition of expensive mistakes, stale memory, changed code/graph context and loss of useful experience between runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: maintenance/discovery model specialists judge evidence-backed guidance changes; host validates cost/provenance, revisions and scope before publication. - Decision owner: standard-distribution postmortem model specialists in separate sessions, automatically enabled by default when Active Memory scopes and postmortem remain enabled; fixed validation/SQLite machinery enforces their decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool receipt capture, post-run outcome latch, per-scope indexes, strict cost threshold, version/CAS, bounded context, admission validation, tombstones, retrieval budgets and native hooks. - Closure path: run evidence/tool receipts → postmortem specialist review/discovery → validated revised scoped lessons → durable store → future run retrieval/injection → later model work and next postmortem reassessment. - Boundary reachability: the built-in Active Memory guide explicitly documents default enabled local/Docker paths; postmortem.rs, native supervisor, retrieval and hooks are source-backed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specialist agents exercise context-sensitive keep/adapt/retire/discover discretion from actual evidence; host-only ranking/CAS cannot independently decide what lesson remains useful for future work. - Evidence: Active Memory; postmortem.rs; reconciliation tests; hooks.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: postmortem may abstain when evidence is inadequate, can fail independently of completed main graph, and requires working model/harness hooks. Evidence of a closed adaptive mechanism is not proof that a given lesson causally improves future task quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate ultimate-identity/policy conflict adjudicator and authoritative return loop exists within a graph-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime scopes, model choices, graph contracts, delivery authorization and user approvals are fixed constraints rather than identity-level questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent or parent actor is first-party delegated ultimate purpose/identity choice at this recursion. - Decision owner: user/operator defines graph, tasks, credentials, publishing authority and scope; native runtime enforces these settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph validation, authored runtime plan, capsules, limits, run ledger, Git/CI policy and memory namespace controls. - Closure path: an operator can change configuration for later runs, but no outstanding identity-policy matter is routed to a legitimate S5 authority and returned through an explicit governance closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph profile names, model persona, user delivery ratification, or managed platform policy do not by themselves constitute identity or ultimate-policy decisions. - Evidence: README; graph reference; Active Memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0f8636f6b26e2e8f854dc02dd6102ef0ac8a32ec/assessments/ze/zeroshot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0f8636f6b26e2e8f854dc02dd6102ef0ac8a32ec\">0f8636f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:33:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/desplega-ai/agent-swarm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Swarm</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/desplega-ai/agent-swarm/tree/65e0ab6bb1f50f796b9c226e2bb34b5041cbaa66\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65e0ab6bb1f50f796b9c226e2bb34b5041cbaa66\">65e0ab6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Operational delivery by distinct coding/research/review workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended implementation/research requests, uncertain tool results and task failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose task-local investigation, tool actions, implementation and completion/progress response. - Decision owner: The model-driven operational worker in the configured provider session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runner prompt assembly, provider adapters, MCP tools, task persistence and runtime limits transport/enforce worker decisions. - Closure path: Task → assembled context → worker tool/action choices → progress/result → task and lead follow-up. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model worker leaves scheduling/storage, not the same discretionary investigation or implementation. - Evidence: docker-compose.example.yml; src/commands/runner.ts (adapter.createSession and task handling); templates/official/coder/CLAUDE.md; src/prompts/base-prompt.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Provider access is required; the assessment does not import unexamined provider governance or claim successful business output.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference among concurrent S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New requests arrive through different ingresses while related work remains in flight, risking duplicate or contradictory implementation/review. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret sibling intent, coordinate with named workers, avoid duplicate action, and choose compatible task work. - Decision owner: Lead and operational model workers own the coordination judgment; the sibling detector does not decide task semantics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: contextKey lookup, in-flight task status, original-intent rendering, messaging/task tools, same-agent parent-session selection and lead no-duplicate rules. - Closure path: Same-context sibling discovery → task prompt lists owners/status/original requests and instructs coordinate/do not duplicate → agent queries/messages/reuses ongoing work → subsequent assignment/action changes. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sibling context intentionally exposes this inter-worker disturbance; the agent decides what overlaps and what should change. - Evidence: src/tasks/sibling-awareness.ts; src/tasks/sibling-block.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md rules 2, 7, 8; src/tools/send-task.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Structural disturbance witness, not a claim that every collision is prevented. Incorrect/missing context keys can miss related work. - Distinct S1 units: Separately assigned coder, researcher and reviewer workers in the official multi-worker deployment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent same-context tasks from multiple ingresses can duplicate or conflict with each other&#39;s unfinished work; same-PR reviews can be double-assigned. - Attenuating coordination relation: Named sibling tasks and status are supplied to the new worker; explicit coordinate/do-not-duplicate instructions plus task inspection/messaging let it adjust alongside lead deduplication. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Coordination changes which work a new task performs and whether lead creates/routes another task; same-agent continuation can resume existing context. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party renderer explicitly detects concurrent related work, describes its original intent and instructs interference attenuation. Generic mailboxes or dependsOn alone are not the witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control the team&#39;s inside-and-now commitments and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled sessions, failed workers, unclaimed recovery pins, affinity-starved tasks and abandoned commitments across the team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assess current state and choose reroute, retry, cancel, recreate or intervention rather than blindly continue. - Decision owner: The official lead agent owns discretionary current-control decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Heartbeat preflight and code triage detect/escalate; snapshots, task creation, steer/cancel/send tools enforce the selected decision. - Closure path: Whole-team status and standing orders → lead heartbeat/reroute-decision task or worker-failure follow-up → lead investigates and chooses corrective task/resource action → task service changes subsequent worker operation. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatic retry thresholds and transactions expose exceptional state; lead must choose the organizational response after those mechanisms cannot settle it. - Evidence: src/heartbeat/heartbeat.ts; src/heartbeat/templates.ts; templates/official/lead/HEARTBEAT.md; src/tasks/worker-follow-up.ts; src/tools/templates.ts task.worker.failed; src/tools/steer-task.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: A parent can administer deployment, but isolated configuration or approval is not counted as a distinct evidenced parent S3 mode. - Whole-system current view: Gathered agents, active/pending/failed tasks and stalled sessions, combined with lead standing orders and full task-detail access. - Current-control decision scope: Team commitments and recovery: cancel obsolete work, reassign eligible workers, steer current execution, recreate still-needed failed work and avoid retry storms.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementary audit of an operational code-delivery claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coder can report completion while code contains security/race/state-machine faults or inadequate evidence/tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Form an independent review judgment with blocking findings/request-changes and return corrective requirements. - Decision owner: A distinct official reviewer model session owns audit judgment; coder/lead own correction and subsequent work routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate worker environment/task, direct git checkout, GitHub review/CI access, task completion/follow-up and parentTaskId continuity. - Closure path: Lead routes a review to reviewer → reviewer independently clones/checks out actual PR code and examines tests/CI → blocking findings/request changes → coder resolves feedback with parentTaskId continuity; lead can route corrective work. Human approval remains a separate merge requirement. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer directly inspects operational evidence in a separate role/session and decides substantive findings, beyond the implementer&#39;s summary or deterministic CI verdict. - Evidence: docker-compose.example.yml; templates/official/reviewer/CLAUDE.md; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md routing/review rules; templates/official/coder/CLAUDE.md review-feedback rule; src/tasks/worker-follow-up.ts; src/tools/templates.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: This is a supported role-based audit path when lead assigns review, not proof every task is audited. The ordinary 500-character completion follow-up alone is not complementary audit; shared credentials/model family are not institutional independence. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s proposed change is ready/correct and tests cover changed paths. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder progress/completion and a summary follow-up task to lead. - Complementary access path: Reviewer must clone repository and check out the PR branch, inspect actual code, tests and CI; not only read the coder&#39;s summary/diff. - Independence boundary: Separate reviewer worker/session and instructions, distinct from the producing coder; official deployment includes the reviewer. - Who acts on findings: Reviewer requests changes with concrete blocking fixes; coder resolves review feedback in the originating task context, with lead routing and subsequent review as needed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Outside-and-then intelligence for the deployed team&#39;s own GTM campaign. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing discovery channels, search demand and content opportunities make the team&#39;s existing GTM plan less effective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Turn external trend distinctions into candidate future campaign/content adaptations; adopter must still compose selection and implementation closure. - Decision owner: The configured lead produces options; deployment owner must supply campaign goal, integrations and the decisive adoption/return-to-S3 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party parameterized weekly schedule prompt, GitHub/GSC integrations, configured GTM plan/state, admin report and saved research workspace. - Closure path: Configured own-campaign metrics → week-over-week search/referrer/content analysis → what-to-try-next and gap/opportunity options → report. Turning that report into an adopted current-capability change is intentionally not supplied as an automatic closed mode. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A function-specific first-party prospective adaptation constructor exists, but no closed agent-owned or parent-owned adoption loop is established by the template. - Evidence: templates/schedules/gtm-weekly-review/config.json; templates/schedules/gtm-weekly-review/content.md; templates/official/discoverability-optimizer/SOUL.md. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Optional template; requires own GTM goal, REPOURL/GSCPROPERTY and integrations. Ordinary customer research as a delivered task, generic memory, persona edits and arbitrary extensions are not S4 witnesses. - External distinction: Repository traffic/referrers/popular paths and Search Console queries, impressions, CTR and rankings for the deployment&#39;s configured campaign. - Future / prospective distinction: Week-over-week change, high-impression low-CTR/rank opportunities, content gaps and what to try next against the current GTM plan. - Adaptation option generated: Candidate SEO/content/campaign experiments to improve the team&#39;s own future reach. - Path back into current capability / S3: Report through configured admin/in-app channels and saved research workspace intentionally exposes options to the deployment; actor authority and adoption/execution closure must be composed, so C rather than A/P.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity and ultimate-policy adjudication at deployed-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A prospective conflict about the team&#39;s constitutive purpose or ultimate policy would require authoritative settlement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive identity/ultimate-policy arbitration and return loop is supplied in the inspected deployed surfaces. - Decision owner: No in-boundary S5 owner established; operator configuration and lead profile editing are not sufficient evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL/IDENTITY/CLAUDE persistence, update-profile, repo guidelines, permission/budget and extension activation controls. - Closure path: Observed paths persist persona, operational checks and permissions; they do not reconstruct a disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter being authoritatively settled and governing future operation. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Neither writable identity files nor final human say on individual merges establishes the S5 function. - Evidence: docs-site/content/docs/(documentation)/architecture/agents.mdx/architecture/agents.mdx); src/tools/update-profile.ts; src/tools/repos/update-repo.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md; src/prompts/base-prompt.ts; docs-site/content/docs/(documentation)/guides/extensions.mdx/guides/extensions.mdx). - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Bounded absence in this deployed distribution, not an impossibility claim for a separately composed organization&#39;s governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/548479149bdce4a226ecd139c0a8c4f7f244b2c7/assessments/ag/agent-swarm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 548479149bdce4a226ecd139c0a8c4f7f244b2c7\">5484791 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T03:07:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gas Town</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown/tree/649b832b7672bc7a2dbef26f5983aba6198b819b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 649b832b7672bc7a2dbef26f5983aba6198b819b\">649b832 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement bounded software-engineering issues in project repositories and return completed branch/work results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, repository state, implementation choices, tool/test observations, local blockers and discovered defects within an assigned issue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive implementation/research/debugging actions required to satisfy the hooked issue and decide when the bounded work is ready to submit through the prescribed completion path. - Decision owner: the model-driven Polecat worker agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated git worktrees, hooked Beads assignments, role/formula context, tool-capable model runtime, direct issue state updates and gt done completion plumbing. - Closure path: Mayor/runtime hooks an issue to a Polecat → Polecat executes autonomously in its isolated worktree → commits/pushes and runs the completion protocol → branch/result enters the downstream Witness/Refinery or configured PR path. - Boundary reachability: Polecat role templates, worktree provisioning, hook state and completion commands are shipped first-party Gas Town surfaces at the pinned revision; the external model host supplies inference but does not replace Gas Town&#39;s first-party worker contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role and formula constrain execution, but the model-driven worker chooses substantive code/tool actions and how to solve the assigned issue. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/polecat.md.tmpl, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate integration interference among independently produced Polecat branches so concurrent work does not collide on a stale common baseline or land in an incompatible order. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple autonomous workers can finish branches from earlier target states; parallel/direct merging can create conflicts, invalid dependency order or inconsistent integration as the target branch moves after each accepted change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next branch/merge order using priority, dependencies and timing; rebase it against the current target; resolve or reject conflicts; and require each subsequent branch to rebase against the newly advanced target. - Decision owner: the model-driven Refinery agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-rig merge queue, MERGEREADY protocol, isolated worker branches, sequential-rebase rule, target-resolution rules and merge-request state. - Closure path: multiple Polecat outputs enter the queue → Refinery selects one and rebases it on the current target → accepted merge advances the target → the next queued branch is rebased against that new baseline; unresolvable conflicts reopen the source issue and return through Witness for new work. - Boundary reachability: the canonical Refinery role and merge queue are shipped first-party operating surfaces for non-fork rigs; the role contract explicitly assigns merge/conflict/order judgment to the running agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/state machinery exposes and enforces the integration surface, but the first-party Refinery contract gives the model agent discretion over ordering and conflict/failure disposition; this is not merely authored static sequencing. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/refinery.md.tmpl, docs/design/mail-protocol.md, internal/refinery/batch.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: isolated worktrees by themselves would only be supporting interference attenuation; the A classification rests on the agent-owned sequential integration feedback loop. - Distinct S1 units: two or more autonomous Polecats completing separate issues/branches in the same rig or integration target. - Inter-S1 disturbance: their branches can be based on the same earlier target and therefore conflict, invalidate each other&#39;s assumptions or require dependency-sensitive ordering when integrated. - Attenuating coordination relation: Refinery serially selects, rebases and integrates branches against the current moving target instead of allowing parallel direct merges. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: failed/conflicting work reopens its source issue and returns through Witness for re-sling/rework; successful integration changes the baseline against which later queued work is evaluated. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited relation addresses a concrete interference mode among outputs of independent S1 workers — incompatible concurrent branch integration — and feeds conflict/failure results back into later operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the town&#39;s current work commitments, dispatch, cross-rig allocation, escalations and active execution on behalf of the whole Gas Town workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unassigned ready work, stalled or failed workers, competing/cross-rig work, blocked dependencies, changed priorities, integration/escalation problems and idle or misallocated execution capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what work to dispatch and to which rig/worker, coordinate batch/cross-rig work, resolve escalations that Witnesses cannot handle, redirect priorities/integration decisions and intervene when current agents or rigs are stuck. - Decision owner: the model-driven Mayor agent in the standard autonomous mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: town/rig status, convoys, Beads dependencies, mail/escalation state, Polecat lists, hooks, gt sling, scheduler capacity support and Witness/Deacon feedback. - Closure path: current town/rig/convoy/escalation state becomes visible to Mayor → Mayor makes dispatch/coordination/intervention decisions → Gas Town creates/hooks workers or routes instructions/escalations → Polecat/Witness/Refinery/Deacon behavior proceeds under the changed current state and reports back. - Boundary reachability: Gas Town ships a dedicated Mayor session, first-party Mayor role template and control commands; Mayor is launched as an agent and connected to the same persisted work/communication surfaces used by the operational organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scheduler and hook mechanisms constrain execution, but the global dispatch, cross-rig judgment and escalation-resolution rights are assigned to the model-driven Mayor rather than being fully predetermined by runtime code. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/cmd/mayor.go, README.md, docs/design/escalation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the human Overseer can interact with or administratively operate Gas Town, but the reviewed evidence does not establish a sufficiently explicit distinct parent S3 operating mode comparable to a first-party approval/override lifecycle; ordinary human tasking and process controls are therefore not encoded as A(P). - Whole-system current view: Mayor has first-party commands and persisted state for overall town status, rigs, Polecats, active convoys, issue/dependency readiness, mail and escalations across the workspace. - Current-control decision scope: current work dispatch, worker/rig routing, batch/cross-rig coordination, escalation resolution, priority/integration judgments and intervention on stuck execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a Polecat&#39;s completion claim before its branch is allowed to land and return corrective action when the integrated result is not acceptable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-verification can miss failures that appear only after rebasing onto the current target, integration conflicts, branch-caused test failures or untracked pre-existing failures that would otherwise be merged without an independent gate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: run the configured verification suite after rebase, decide whether failure is branch-caused or pre-existing, block/reject a failing branch, reopen its source issue and require returned rework, or allow a verified branch to merge. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven Refinery agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MERGEREADY handoff from Witness, separate Refinery session/role, queue state, rebase onto current target, configured setup/typecheck/lint/build/test commands, mandatory handle-failures gate and MERGEFAILED/MERGED return protocol. - Closure path: Polecat completion passes through Witness → Refinery obtains the branch independently and rebases/tests it → pass permits merge; branch-caused failure reopens source issue, closes the merge request and notifies Witness → Witness/current-control machinery can re-sling corrected operational work. - Boundary reachability: the verification gate is embedded in the shipped canonical Refinery patrol contract and directly controls whether standard non-fork work reaches the target branch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tests provide evidence but do not decide the organizational response; Refinery independently interprets conflict/failure provenance and owns the block/reopen/merge judgment under a role explicitly separated from application development. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/refinery.md.tmpl, docs/design/mail-protocol.md, internal/templates/roles/witness.md.tmpl. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the same Refinery actor also owns S2 integration ordering, but the S3 claim uses a distinct decision right: independent acceptance/challenge of a producer completion claim and corrective return, not merge sequencing itself. - Claim being audited: that a Polecat&#39;s submitted branch is integration-safe and satisfies the configured quality/test checks sufficiently to land on the target branch. - Ordinary reporting path: Polecat performs its own completion/self-verification and signals done; Witness processes completion/lifecycle state and sends MERGEREADY. - Complementary access path: Refinery independently checks the queued branch after rebasing onto the current target and runs the configured verification commands in its own merge-processing session. - Independence boundary: Refinery is a separate agent role and is explicitly forbidden from acting as the application developer; it does not simply accept the producer&#39;s self-report and can block landing/reopen work. - Who acts on findings: Refinery directly blocks or merges; on failure it reopens the source issue and notifies Witness, which can return the work to a new/current operational worker.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no materially separate first-party external/prospective adaptation function is established at the reviewed town boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external ecosystem change, future project/runtime conditions, model/provider evolution and strategic capability adaptation were inspected, but no qualifying shipped S4 owner and closed return loop was found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that continuously or episodically distinguishes outside/future conditions, develops adaptation options and returns a selected organizational adaptation into present Gas Town capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mayor strategic/integration judgments, shared memories, project/research documents, HOP/Gas City material, scheduler state and historical capability/ledger data can inform work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: no qualifying outside/future sensing → adaptation option → present capability/current-control return path is established in the credited standard operating surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Mayor&#39;s use of the word strategic concerns current architecture, priority and integration decisions in the reviewed role contract; without a distinct external/prospective sensing-and-adaptation loop it is not promoted to S4. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/cmd/mayor.go, README.md, reviewed design/research surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no materially separate first-party organizational identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the declared Gas Town workspace boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user intent, architecture/priorities, role templates, directives/configuration and Overseer interaction were inspected, but no qualifying town-level identity dispute/policy authority with a closed S5 return path was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for choosing or ratifying the ultimate purpose/identity/constitutional policy of the Gas Town organization as distinct from assigning work, configuring roles or exercising current operational control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the human Overseer, Mayor interface, role/formula templates, project configuration, directives/overlays, rig lifecycle controls and user instructions shape the system but are not sufficient S5 ownership by themselves. - Closure path: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision lifecycle comparable to goal/constitution/strategy ratification and return into autonomous operation was found at the pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the human as Overseer, routing strategic questions to Mayor, or allowing an operator to configure/stop/dock agents does not by itself establish S5; those paths are tasking, configuration or current control unless the ultimate organizational identity decision is explicit. - Evidence: internal/cmd/mayor.go, internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/templates/roles/deacon.md.tmpl, reviewed configuration/directive surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7e14b6da80b9c974758f325654da2d00a7785624/assessments/gastown.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7e14b6da80b9c974758f325654da2d00a7785624\">7e14b6d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T19:14:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Muvon/octomind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Octomind</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Muvon/octomind/tree/fceb8cbe970d2ad097e59d266ae915a3ebb43779\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fceb8cbe970d2ad097e59d266ae915a3ebb43779\">fceb8cb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user objectives through model-directed local/MCP tool actions in an adaptive session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source tree, changing command results, failed tests, tool errors, external API responses and task-specific observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses the next tool, argument and work step after receiving prior action output. - Decision owner: first-party specialist actor in Octomind&#39;s session/chat runtime; the selected external model supplies inference, not a separate vendor harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: native Rust chat session, MCP call dispatch, role/tool config, session history, guardrails, compression, supervision and provider client. - Closure path: task→session model request→model tool instruction→MCP/native executor→recorded tool result→subsequent model turn or terminal result. - Boundary reachability: ordinary installed CLI and piped/daemon session modes enter same first-party model/tool session path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action choices depend on model reasoning about live observations, rather than being only a prewritten deterministic script; policy and tool mediation constrain choices but do not invent them. - Evidence: src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/session/chat/response/toolexecution.rs, src/mcp/core/functions.rs, README.md. - Basis: source-backed installed autonomous specialist. - Confidence: high for runnable first-party code structure, not model task performance. - Caveats: tap-specific tools, hosted model weights and endpoint success are not part of ownership attribution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately demonstrated first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation relation within the selected coding-session and optional specialist group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: nested/dynamic agents can contend for model capacity or shared files; concrete conflicts are plausible but not shown as a closed native feedback relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inter-agent conflict-specific arbitration that changes affected S1 behavior after detecting interference; not established by inspected paths. - Decision owner: supervisor handles the whole session&#39;s plan; child delegation and MCP orchestration communicate tasks/results without proven distinctive inter-S1 collision arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate, MCP agent invocation, monitored tool processes, role constraints, generic authorizer/cost bounds and session/workdir state. - Closure path: child task/return and supervisor&#39;s whole-current plan changes do not independently form an S2-specific conflicting-worker attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a plan owner, tool batch authorization, per-role access bounds or generic delegation do not prove shared-resource contention detection and corrective return between separate operational divisions. - Evidence: src/supervisor/delegate.rs, src/mcp/agent/functions.rs, src/mcp/orchestration/monitor.rs, src/supervisor/plan.rs. - Basis: bounded negative source inspection. - Confidence: moderate at selected recursion level. - Caveats: additional externally configured multi-agent topology might bring S2 evidence, but that would require a concrete first-party collision-specific path and return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: separate model-based whole-current supervisor of active specialist plan/phase commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled work, unverified phase completion, broken assumptions or changed trajectory within a multi-phase objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: external supervisor decides when to create, advance, hold or revise the operative phase plan according to runtime action evidence and original user outcome constraints. - Decision owner: first-party out-of-band supervisor model planner; runtime validates and applies its structured decision. The specialist does not possess the plan mutation tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan signals, per-turn preparation, callsupervisorjson, PlanDecision, sidecarstart/advance/revise, injected authoritative plan notes and bounded error latches. - Closure path: runtime-observed action/phase evidence→external planner decision→persistent current plan transition or evidence-grounded hold→runtime-plan note to model-directed specialist→subsequent task decisions occur under revised current phase. - Boundary reachability: first-party supervisor plan is enabled in shipped default config for qualifying complex tasks; trivial/answer-only jobs intentionally bypass it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct model decides actual whole-task phase regulation and returned current commitments, not a fixed progress counter or human-only approval. A concerns this documented current-control mode only. - Evidence: src/supervisor/plan.rs, src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/mcp/core/functions.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - Whole-system current view: standing instructions, original task, active plan, phase trajectory, evidence ledger and outcome conditions, beyond a single pending model tool call. - Current-control decision scope: choose and revise current task phase commitments and prevent unjustified advancement despite specialist claims. - Basis: source-confirmed distinct autonomous supervisor decision/return. - Confidence: high for structural ownership and default enablement, without live demonstration. - Caveats: planner intentionally declines simple requests, failed model calls can leave bookkeeping unchanged, and no autonomy is imported from a third-party agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: out-of-band supervisor-model verification of producer specialist&#39;s claimed done/completion against independent runtime action evidence and artifacts, with returned corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, missing tests, unsupported work claims and regressions hidden by optimistic specialist narration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately tasked verifier determines Pass/Gaps/Indeterminate, with challenged findings able to prevent verified completion and re-enter correction. - Decision owner: distinct Octomind supervisor verification model invocation (not the specialist&#39;s own status token); first-party runtime owns the binding verdict gate and reentry budget. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime action ledger, readback output, task/claim, ground-truth Git state, structured decision schema, independent refutation, bounded reentry and learning outcome labels. - Closure path: specialist self-reports done→first-party runtime captures actions/artifacts→separate supervisor verifier judges them→Gaps returned to specialist for bounded correction or explicit unverified outcome; Pass finalizes verifiable claim/plan and logs verified outcome. - Boundary reachability: enabled supervisor/gate in packaged default TOML, called at qualifying actual completion claims in the session executor; not off-box CI or benchmark scoring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct model performs substantive correctness judgment with access to runtime-owned evidence not controlled by producer, rather than mere transcript logging or same-agent self-review. - Evidence: src/supervisor/gate.rs, src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/supervisor/detect.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - Claim being audited: requested coding task is actually completed and outcome conditions hold. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist done token, user-facing summary and tool outputs. - Complementary access path: separate supervisor builds task/evidence packet from session action ledger, Git state and tool readbacks; can independently request missing output. - Independence boundary: independent role/model call and runtime-furnished ledger rather than specialist self-report, though same configured model-provider infrastructure may serve both. - Who acts on findings: runtime applies completion gate, new specialist correction prompt or unverified handoff; successful gate finalizes plan and supplies outcome credit. - Basis: source-confirmed agent decision and binding first-party corrective return. - Confidence: high for wired audit constructor, live model quality untested. - Caveats: abstention/timeout is not evidence of passing; no claim that an independent reviewer actually ran in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: when operator enables optional evolution, external and cross-session observations are synthesized into proposed changes of future-session skills/guardrails; first-party counterfactual evaluation determines trial/promotion/retirement of generated behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurrent user constraints, observed repeated failure patterns, newly learned project rules and changed usefulness/cost of optional capabilities across future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate supervisor model may autonomously synthesize an advisory skill candidate from grounded lessons; a first-party counterfactual trial judges whether activating it improves future outcomes, promotes an effective candidate or retires weak/regressed behavior. Parent controls enablement, effectful trials and explicit approval/reject/rollback. - Decision owner: detached learning supervisor plus native promotion/pruning policy own the autonomous S4 path; operator supplies initial opt-in and effectful authorizations, so supplementary parent (P) is explicitly recorded. User approval is not confused with autonomous model judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source-quote and outcome-grounded Lesson, per-project store + scope, synthesizeafterextraction/synthesizestoreifdue, schema verification/replay checks, candidate registry, Shadow→Trial→Active/Retired, held-out counterfactual control/treatment, initforsession and generated skill/guardrail runtime merges. - Closure path: verified previous-session evidence→deduped reusable lessons→separate supervisor chooses a capability candidate→native schema/replay validation→shadow sample of future tasks without application→gated live treatment with counterfactual results→promoted skill/guard accessible to new sessions (or retired candidate)→actual future behavior changes and continues receiving evidence. - Boundary reachability: this is installed first-party [supervisor.learning.evolution] enabled = true mode, off by default, with code from extraction to runtime activation; no unpublished external R&amp;D process or user-installed extension is required. Effectful guards need explicit operator authorization; advisory promotion can be autonomous once opted in. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prospective hypothesis/capability selection is produced by a model outside an individual current-task coding loop, then evaluated and fed back into later operational capabilities. It is not mere text-only memory recall or compaction. Both autonomous and parent-governed decisions have distinct documented roles. - Evidence: src/supervisor/learning/evolution/synthesize.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/runtime.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/registry.rs, src/supervisor/learning/extract.rs, src/session/context.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - External distinction: observations gathered across past sessions and project/role boundaries rather than an in-flight same task&#39;s tool result. - Future / prospective distinction: choose whether a machine-generated reusable skill/guard should persist as available behavior for later tasks. - Adaptation option generated: scope-bounded, verified native skill/guardrail candidate with a registry identity and explicit artifact path. - Path back into current capability / S3: runtime initforsession loads active generated skills and merges validated guardrails; promotion/retirement changes subsequent specialist behavior while gate/plan continue regulating current work. The operator may approve effectful candidates or revert the registry state. - Basis: first-party optional autonomous skill-generation→trial→activation and bounded parent rights. - Confidence: medium-high for source-confirmed pipeline; actual learning benefit not measured. - Caveats: evolution disabled in default config; without activation/grounded lessons or enough verified samples no positive rollout occurs. Distinguish confirmed code path from successful on-device trial. Parent permission for effectful native behaviors is mandatory.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimately constituted ultimate identity and organizational-purpose policymaker beyond scoped user/role constraints within an Octomind session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool misuse, role conflicts and evolved guardrail risks are addressed by operator instructions, bounded security policy and optional supervisor authorizer but do not constitute a separate S5 mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret and resolve top-level organizational identity, constitutional purpose and ultimate contested policy with binding return to S3/S4; not established. - Decision owner: external operator provides objectives/configuration, first-party security controller enforces those constraints, supervisor authorizer may optionally judge specific tool intent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role settings, hard guards, user-intent authorizer, command confirmation, generated-effectful authorization and user approval. - Closure path: a disallowed tool can be blocked, but no separately chartered ultimate organization policy/identity judgment returns to govern system aims; parent S4 guard approval does not create one. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy-as-code and delegated parent permissions are not sufficient to demonstrate a legitimately differentiated S5 above the task supervisor. - Evidence: src/supervisor/authorizer.rs, src/session/guardrails.rs, src/config/mod.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/runtime.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user or adopting organization might own external S5, but that is not a first-party installed control loop at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae89fd48a07e9d2be6e67d313d1dfd935d61c95f/assessments/octomind.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae89fd48a07e9d2be6e67d313d1dfd935d61c95f\">ae89fd4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T17:09:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blade Code</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code/tree/30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\">30f8684 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform software-engineering and automation work through model-selected repository/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, tool/test/browser failures, model/provider variation, context pressure and bounded delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret returned evidence, decide when to delegate or create a team and decide when the assigned operational objective is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Blade Code agent and, for delegated bounded work, first-party subagent/team-member agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/executor, permissions, context/session runtime, provider adapters, task admission, goal state, worktrees and cancellation. - Closure path: user/parent goal → agent chooses direct or delegated coding action → first-party runtime executes → tool/subagent/team evidence returns → agent revises work → completion is accepted when runtime completion gates permit it. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI/Web/Headless/ACP runtime uses the same first-party Agent/tool machinery; Task and Team tools are shipped model-callable surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and constrains actions, but the model chooses open-ended implementation, investigation, delegation and completion decisions. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/Agent.ts; packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/task/task.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permissions and scheduling can delay/block actions without becoming the owner of the engineering decision itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrent operational teammates by assigning dependencies/ownership and isolating write-capable workstreams while coordinating peer progress. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate task claiming, dependency-order violations, concurrent workers editing overlapping workspace state, and peer work that must be synchronized before downstream tasks proceed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the team members, task partition, dependency graph, initial assignments and whether work benefits from parallel specialization/peer review; use peer messages to adjust coordination as execution unfolds. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamCreate/SendMessage, with deterministic task-graph/worktree mechanisms enforcing the selected relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamTaskGraph, atomic claim, automatic unblock, durable peer mailbox, managed worktree isolation for write-capable members and worktree lifecycle/merge machinery. - Closure path: lead identifies separable/interdependent work → creates roles/tasks/dependencies → teammates claim only unblocked work and write-capable members default to isolated worktrees → completion/unblocking/messages update peer execution → downstream units proceed under the adjusted coordination state. - Boundary reachability: TeamCreate, TeamTaskClaim, SendMessage and managed teammate worktrees are shipped first-party runtime tools, not documentation-only concepts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces claims, dependencies and isolation, but the lead agent chooses the substantive organizational partition and dependency structure. - Distinct S1 units: persistent model-backed teammates/subagents that own bounded implementation or review outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent workers can duplicate work, violate ordering, or collide through shared writes; managed worktrees and dependency/claim rules exist specifically to reduce those interactions. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead-selected DAG/assignment plus atomic task claiming, automatic dependency unblocking, durable peer messaging and worktree isolation for writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: completed tasks unblock dependent work; peer messages enter running teammate turns; task ownership/claim state changes which unit may work next; isolated results are later integrated rather than concurrently overwriting the same checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanisms regulate concrete interaction among simultaneously viable operational workers rather than merely transporting a delegated prompt. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamTaskGraph.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/SubagentWorktreeLifecycle.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: atomic claiming and worktree mechanics are deterministic support; autonomy is credited to the lead&#39;s model-owned organizational decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a live whole-team view and regulate current operational commitments while the multi-agent organization is running. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled or unwanted teammates, changed current priorities, task/member status changes, blocked downstream work and a team that should be stopped after synthesis or failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the live team/member/task graph, steer a running teammate with a direct message, broadcast a changed instruction, and terminate running teammates by deleting the current team. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamStatus, SendMessage and TeamDelete. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSessionStore-backed live member state, durable task graph, TeamMailbox, running-turn message delivery and team/member cancellation. - Closure path: lead inspects TeamStatus/current graph → detects a current execution need → sends targeted/broadcast steering or terminates the team → running teammate/session state changes → later TeamStatus/results reflect the intervention and the lead proceeds accordingly. - Boundary reachability: TeamStatus/SendMessage/TeamDelete are shipped model-callable first-party tools available to the team lead. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: live state storage and cancellation transport are deterministic, but the lead model interprets whole-current state and decides the substantive intervention. - Whole-system current view: TeamStatus returns live member state from AgentSessionStore together with durable task-graph state for the owned team. - Current-control decision scope: current peer steering, task/role supervision through the shared graph, and cancellation of running team commitments when work should stop. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamRuntime.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamMailbox.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic stop/cancel would not be enough alone; the positive claim rests on the combined live whole-team status surface plus targeted running-turn steering and commitment termination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge qualifying implementation work with a fresh read-only verifier whose evidence can block completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect implementation claims, failing tests/types/lint, medium/high-risk defects, race/security issues and author self-verification bias. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently execute allowed verification commands, inspect the authoritative changed-file scope and emit structured PASS/FAIL/PARTIAL findings. - Decision owner: the separately instantiated built-in verification agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh subagent context, read-only tool restrictions, authoritative verification prompt, mutation-revision tracking, bounded retry gate and deterministic completion policy. - Closure path: main agent changes qualifying implementation → runtime requires fresh verification Task → independent verifier gathers direct project/tool evidence and emits verdict → FAIL/PARTIAL forces repair/re-verification; only fresh PASS for current mutation revision permits completion. - Boundary reachability: independentVerification is wired into the shipped main loop and explicitly requires the built-in verification subagent for qualifying changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the audit judgment from its own evidence path; deterministic runtime code enforces freshness and blocks completion when the verdict is insufficient. - Claim being audited: the main coding agent&#39;s claim that the current non-trivial implementation is correct and ready to finish. - Ordinary reporting path: the author agent&#39;s normal tool results, implementation narrative and self-checks. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only verifier runs project checks and reads changed files directly under an authoritative scope. - Independence boundary: fresh model-backed subagent, no write tools, no nested subagents, tool-evidence-only instructions and no reliance on the author&#39;s claimed test outcome. - Who acts on findings: the main agent must repair reported failures and invoke a new verification run; the runtime completion gate prevents success until the required PASS is fresh. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/loop/independentVerification.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/verification.md; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/builtinVerificationAgent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic code-review skills are not used for this claim; the positive S3 path is the enforced built-in verifier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying future/external condition is placed under a function that develops adaptation options and returns them into current S3 capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auto-memory, compaction-time consolidation, provider health/recovery, web/browser tools, goals, schedules and plugin/skill configuration can affect later work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory consolidation extracts preferences/conventions/lessons/debugging notes and injects retained project knowledge into later sessions; that is persistence/learning context rather than an evidenced outside-and-then option-development conversation with current S3. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/memory/AutoMemoryManager.ts; packages/cli/src/memory/MemoryConsolidation.ts; packages/cli/src/goals/executionFrontier.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the runtime has substantial persistence and external tooling, but these do not satisfy the Methodology 0.3.6 external-and-prospective option loop without an adaptation owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is routed to a distinct authoritative S5 owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes, workspace trust, system prompts, tool policy, plugin/MCP security and configuration constrain execution but do not create identity-governance closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Blade Code agents operate within developer/user-authored constraints and do not have authority to redefine the system&#39;s ultimate identity or governing principles. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/config/PermissionChecker.ts; packages/cli/src/security/WorkspaceTrustService.ts; packages/cli/src/prompts/sections.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator permissions and configuration are legitimate constraints, not identity/ultimate-policy governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96/assessments/blade-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96\">37d2b8f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T23:52:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SICA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent/tree/ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\">ed8275d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: First-party agent loops perform model-directed coding/source inspection, sandboxed shell operations, benchmark problem solving and independently executing delegated workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New bug/task descriptions, mutable repository content, tool errors, model output and local experiment conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model selects concrete coding steps and allowed tools after receiving tool outputs or prior subagent results. - Decision owner: Executing CodingAgent/ProblemSolver child model, MainOrchestrator&#39;s bounded solver actions and base agent tool-call continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent classes, tool parser and call graph, executecommand, file and edit tools, sandbox Docker process. - Closure path: Task → model-directed operations → actual tool/command outputs and artifact → subsequent step or returned result, with sandbox limits. - Boundary reachability: Packaged baseagent.agent runs MainOrchestratorAgent, which can create coding/model workers; shipped runner.py invokes that executable in isolated Docker containers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task-specific coding decisions are model-generated rather than only an external benchmark script. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/coder.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py; baseagent/src/tools/executecommand.py; runner.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent distinct concurrent benchmark-running agent S1 workers from interfering through shared host HTTP ports and shared benchmark result-tracker mutation. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple Python/Docker child coding agents each solve separate benchmark problems through runbenchmarkswithjobqueue and processjob, independently maintaining a coding task loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: If two independently executing benchmark agents bind the same host port 8080/808x, one container fails to start; concurrent writes to the same benchmark JSONL tracker may lose or corrupt result/progress state. - Attenuating coordination relation: runbenchmarkswithjobqueue allocates a unique workerid from bounded idqueue; processjob binds container host port 808{workerid}. Each benchmark uses asyncio.Lock around tracker start/result updates, preventing simultaneous writes to shared provenance. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each worker occupies one reserved ID/port while running its model and returns ID only on exit; subsequent agent work waits for a safe slot or starts on another allocated ID; results can be safely saved and analyzed for next iteration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party ID lease and tracker lock address two named interference modes produced by distinct simultaneously acting model agents; the evidence goes beyond mere task assignment/queue routing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Host port contention and cross-worker result-store writes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate/release exclusive host port and serialize benchmark tracker mutations. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic idqueue and per-benchmark asyncio.Lock constructor, not an autonomous S2 mediator model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded maxworkers, unique Docker names, per-problem workdir mounts and benchmark data lock. - Closure path: Several runnable problem jobs → lease workerid/unique host port → parallel model sessions → guarded tracker write → release lease → next job. - Boundary reachability: Default runner.py uses this first-party benchmark job runner during evaluation of each agent iteration; positive S2 is bounded to this concurrent benchmark-agent mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Safe exclusive-resource scheduling is deterministic host-owned (C), not the meta agent&#39;s discretionary choice. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/benchmarks/base.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven executive direction of multiple current subordinate coding workers with shared task progress and corrective redirection based on their execution results. - Whole-system current view: MainOrchestrator owns the top-level task and receives the complete problem and a synthesized live execution tree from CallGraphManager after subordinate calls, including already attempted work/results and current subordinate execution state. - Current-control decision scope: MainOrchestrator selects/decomposes active subagent tasks, assigns complete subtask instructions, scrutinizes their returned evidence, changes the plan, commissions further workers/verification and decides when a coherent current task result is submitted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagent failures, incomplete work, duplicate approaches, requirement mismatch and uncertain current progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Discretionary model-controlled reassignment, continuation/stop and verification of the current multi-agent goal; no reliance solely on fixed queue. - Decision owner: First-party MainOrchestratorAgent LLM (A) at parent/current multiunit recursion, separate from subordinate child-model operational work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CallGraphManager, generated execution-tree event, model-driven agent calls, structured task results, cost/time governors. - Closure path: Current top-level intent → orchestrator decomposition → model worker runs → returned results plus direct callgraph operating state → revised plans/delegation and final task acceptance. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py constructs MainOrchestratorAgent and starts it as the initial executing actor in CLI and benchmark mode; role documented in the agent&#39;s first-party core prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent model uses evidence and discretionary judgment to govern concurrent/successive independently thinking subagent units, rather than merely running a predefined list of subtasks. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate model-driven runtime monitor checks independent live callgraph/effect events for subagent nonprogress, looping and abnormal execution and can issue corrective notices or terminate faulty child work. - Claim being audited: Operational actors&#39; implicit assertions of continuing useful work and healthy nested execution; notably a child may be silently looping or failing while its parent awaits results. - Ordinary reporting path: Each coding child returns self-authored work and final text to its parent, potentially omitting stalled nested activity. - Complementary access path: An independently launched Overseer model periodically/event-triggeredly reads generateoverseerexecutiontree(CallGraphManager), a first-party event-derived live execution graph, not only the child/parent&#39;s narrative result. This is source-observed execution, although not an independently executed code quality benchmark. - Independence boundary: Overseer is a separate model invocation outside producer and supervising parent tool-delegation turn, with its own model option and timing/event observations. Shared first-party callgraph and provider limits remain; no external independent auditor claim. - Who acts on findings: Overseer itself publishes OVERSEERNOTIFICATION to an implicated agent/parent and calls CallGraphManager.cancelagent when model judge sets forcecancelagent; parent responds via next tool/model decision. It also adapts future monitoring cadence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task nonprogress, recursion loops, stalled/erratic worker execution and hidden operational states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model independently decides concern/notifiability, which actor to notify, whether to cancel, and whether future checks are time/event-driven. - Decision owner: Separate autonomous Overseer model, not a rule-only timeout checker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EventBus subscriptions, execution-tree renderer, OverseerJudgement schema, LLM check interval, notifications and cancellation path. - Closure path: Real child event and call graph → separate model Overseer diagnosis → notification or direct cancel → parent agent control loop sees issue and can reassign; subsequent oversight resamples. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py always instantiates and starts Overseer when running packaged agent; its monitoringloop subscribes to tool result/assistant events while root agent executes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit&#39;s judgment and direct cancellation/intervention are separate model decisions using a complementary event-derived operating view; not a producer self-review or passive observability dashboard. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/oversight/overseer.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/reporting.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for wiring, medium for audit effectiveness - Caveats: Overseer is an execution-health complementary audit, not independent code correctness review. It may share model provider and callgraph evidence with producer; cancellation can be mistaken or delayed. ReviewCommittee is separately model-driven design critique but is not credited as direct runtime correctness audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven future-capability adaptation: evaluate externally defined benchmark tasks and past agent-version results, generate and implement software architecture/tool changes for subsequent agent generations. - External distinction: Benchmark tasks and measured grader outcomes originate outside the current coding agent&#39;s self-report; the archive records past failures, costs and utility scores. Published literature/web exploration can inform new feature options in the meta-improvement reasoning workflow. - Future / prospective distinction: The meta agent is tasked to improve the next version&#39;s general coding competence, not just solve the currently evaluated benchmark problem or fix the present code task. - Adaptation option generated: MetaImprovementReasoningStructure directs archive analysis (working and failed features), a model-chosen new feature/strategy, code change proposal, multi-member read-only ReviewCommittee critique, model-directed implementation and planned tests. - Path back into current capability / S3: runner.py evaluates a version, selects a base version by stored benchmark statistics and confidence bound, copies code into next iteration, runs that version&#39;s model-driven improve mode on its own codebase, then rebuilds Docker and runs subsequent version benchmarks; resulting changed tool/prompt/coding policy becomes future subordinate S1 capability used by MainOrchestrator&#39;s current-work organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-term changes in measured coding benchmark performance and repeated feature failures, rather than current tool-loop transient errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses which agent architecture/tool/behavior feature should be changed after comparing archived experience and review committee feedback, with real downstream implementation and retest. - Decision owner: Meta-improvement MainOrchestrator/CodingAgent and archive analysis model in the explicit self-improvement operating mode; version selection/testing is first-party deterministic support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmark registry, archive analyzer with utility/CI, selectbaseagent, independent model-driven ReviewCommittee, meta-improvement reasoning structure, agentchangelog.md, isolated next-agent directory. - Closure path: External benchmark/task outcomes → version-level archive → model diagnosis and novel future improvement option → separate committee critique → actual coding changes to next version → newly launched/evaluated agent iteration and updated archive. - Boundary reachability: Documented python runner.py default cycle executes benchmarks and then runmetaagentbenchmark, which launches baseagent improve mode. That mode integrates meta-improvement reasoning and committee. No external company service is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Future capability choice and proposal/refinement are model-authored, not merely a static benchmark-triggered template or heuristic version selector; first-party script closes deployment/evaluation. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/tools/reasoningstructures/metaimprovement.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/archiveexplorer.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for model-driven improvement cycle, low for empirical net improvement - Caveats: A new iteration is produced even when benchmark gains are not established; selection uses observed statistics but does not prove monotonic improvement, safety or benchmark transfer. No production performance results inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced legitimate ultimate-purpose/policy authority with identity-level deliberation and binding return across all future agent adaptations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risk of benchmark Goodharting, security tradeoffs, resource spending and deciding which future changes are acceptable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Humans configure objectives/budget, benchmark suite, model credentials and Docker trust. Committee can critique designs but cannot ratify ultimate identity or adjudicate mission change. - Decision owner: External experiment operator and developer-set prompt/policy boundaries; no dedicated legitimate S5 body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReviewCommittee &#39;taste maker&#39;, testing manager, fixed guards, benchmark score criterion, sandbox/budget limits. - Closure path: Committee reports recommendations and model may refine next feature; no identity-level issue/ultimate authority/legitimate binding policy return at S5 recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A sophisticated self-improvement S4 or design-review committee does not imply governing constitutional/organizational identity authority. - Evidence: baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; runner.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/reviewcommitteemember.py; baseagent/src/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee/assessments/sica.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee\">3efb20a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T22:28:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CocoPlus</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus/tree/b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\">b252a8e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist/persona agents autonomously perform the data-engineering/project transformations defined by the active CocoPlus goal/flow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requirements, code/data artifacts, tool/Snowflake results, tests, implementation uncertainty and bounded project-state changes within each specialist remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local implementation/analysis/tool actions needed to complete the assigned stage/iteration outcome. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoPlus specialist/producer agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: personas, Snowflake Coco host, phase/stage contracts, model-tier floors, isolated worktrees, artifacts and tool/policy hooks. - Closure path: specialists produce artifacts/evidence in their bounded stage; outputs are persisted and passed to later stage/review/current-control paths, affecting subsequent project work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gates/hook policy restrict actions but do not choose the specialist&#39;s substantive solution; the model-driven actor owns local operational judgment. - Evidence: README.md and CocoForge/CocoFlow documentation describe specialist agents executing build/test/review/project stages and autonomous long-running modes. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate dependencies and destructive concurrent interference among specialist/producer S1 units while preserving useful parallelism. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-invalid stage starts, concurrent producers touching overlapping file scopes, incomplete work contaminating another agent&#39;s context and oscillation between parallelism levels after failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which approved-plan work can run simultaneously versus must wait, and adapt stage parallelism from current dependency/failure conditions. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoHarvest/Team Lead coordination actor operating under the first-party flow contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph/gates, isolated worktrees, disjoint producer filescope, checkpoint artifacts, concurrency/retry budgets and deterministic dispatch guards. - Closure path: the coordination decision changes which stages/producers dispatch, which wait, and the scope they receive; dependency/checkpoint failures and disjoint-scope enforcement prevent unsafe execution until the coordination condition changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dependency/worktree mechanisms enforce safe boundaries, while the agentic harvest/lead path chooses the useful parallelization/coordination response within them. - Evidence: docs/features.html calls CocoHarvest the intelligence behind parallelism and describes dependency-aware waits/isolated worktrees/adaptive stage parallelism; AGENTS.md.template requires concurrent producer scopes to remain disjoint. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: plain phase sequencing is not credited; the positive witness is the concrete dependency/write-contamination disturbance with an agent-owned parallelism decision. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialist/persona/producer agents executing separate CocoFlow/CocoForge/CocoFleet work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writes or dependency-invalid parallel stages can contaminate shared state, create conflicting outputs or cause downstream work to start from incomplete prerequisites. - Attenuating coordination relation: CocoHarvest/Team Lead chooses parallel versus waiting/dependency order; disjoint file scopes, isolated worktrees and checkpoint gates enforce that chosen separation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: only admitted stages/producers dispatch, blocked dependencies remain waiting, and failure/regression signals can narrow parallelism before later execution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is explicitly tied to preventing cross-agent contamination and dependency conflict, not merely assigning or ordering tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current whole-run commitments, specialist dispatch, iteration/milestone progression, budgets, recovery and live intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing iterations, budget pressure, stalled stages, specialist output disagreement, quality-gate failure, current run-policy constraints and exceptions requiring developer intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomous mode — choose iteration plan/dispatch/synthesis/next milestone response and current recovery within hard bounds; parent mode — clear human-gated stages, steer/stop/resume or resolve a dispute when current-control authority is deliberately reserved to the developer. - Decision owner: autonomous mode — model-driven Team Lead/run coordinator; parent mode — developer/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forge-state.json goal/iteration/phase/budget/team/history, milestone gates, run-policy snapshots, stall detection, retry budgets, AGENTSTOP/STEER, human-gate files and deterministic pre-tool policy hooks. - Closure path: autonomous lead decisions change specialist assignments, iteration state and next-stage progression; parent decisions clear/retain gates, resolve disputes or stop/steer the run, and the persisted state/policy governs subsequent agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Team Lead owns current orchestration within the established run boundary; hard hooks enforce constraints, while explicitly documented human gates/dispute/steering paths preserve a separate parent-owned mode rather than being attributed to the runtime. - Evidence: forge.skill.md assigns plan/dispatch/synthesis/milestone-gate decisions to Team Lead and persists whole-run state; AGENTS.md.template supplies AGENTSTOP/STEER, non-bypassable human gates and run-policy boundaries; docs/features.html supplies recovery/escalation/adaptive stage control. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: static policy enforcement by hooks is not credited as S3 ownership; the mapping depends on Team Lead discretion and explicit returned parent decisions. - Whole-system current view: current forge goal, iteration, phase, budget status, team, history, stage dependencies, evidence/critic status and run-policy snapshot. - Current-control decision scope: iteration plan, specialist dispatch, synthesis, milestone progression, current recovery/parallelism and developer-gated intervention.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge specialist/producer quality claims before milestone/signoff using a structurally separated critic path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-approval, rationalization, hidden quality regressions and acceptance of evidence that does not meet stated criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue PASS/REJECT quality judgment from the independent evidence presented to the critic and gate progression accordingly. - Decision owner: model-driven Quality Critic / read-only fleet critic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: critic read-only role, lack of specialist reasoning traces, default-REJECT stance, producer/critic separation, required PASS signoff and milestone/refinement gates. - Closure path: critic verdict enters the milestone/flow state; rejection prevents acceptance/progression and drives another fix iteration/dispute, while PASS permits the governed continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critic agent owns the quality judgment; read-only/separation/gating mechanisms preserve independence and enforce its consequences but do not produce the judgment. - Evidence: AGENTS.md.template states that the Quality Critic receives iteration outputs but not specialist reasoning traces, starts from REJECT and cannot be overruled by Team Lead; fleet mode separates producers from read-only critics and can require PASS; forge.skill.md places critic verdict in each milestone gate. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: routine tests/evidence gates are not the S3 witness; the mapping is specifically the independent critic path with complementary output-level access and non-overridable feedback. - Claim being audited: that specialist/producer iteration output meets the stated goal/quality/evidence criteria and is safe to accept for progression. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist/producer returns its output/artifacts into the Team Lead/flow iteration. - Complementary access path: separate Quality Critic/read-only critic receives iteration outputs/evidence without the specialist reasoning trace and evaluates them under its own criteria/default-REJECT stance. - Independence boundary: critic is separated from producer, read-only in fleet mode, lacks producer reasoning traces and cannot be overruled by Team Lead. - Who acts on findings: Team Lead/flow milestone control consumes PASS/REJECT; rejection forces another iteration, withheld signoff or dispute escalation before progression.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: inspect externally changed model capability after an upgrade, determine how that changes the value of existing governance/harness rules and develop evidence-backed adaptation options for future operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a newer model may make standing rules/checks newly redundant, advisory or still necessary, creating risk of stale harness constraints or unsafe premature deletion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which recurring rule/check should remain load-bearing, degrade to advisory or be proposed as obsolete after model-capability change. - Decision owner: constructor path — CocoPlus supplies the S4-specific model-upgrade hygiene analysis, but the standard path does not autonomously close destructive rule changes; developer composition/authority is still required for returned adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: $hygiene --model-upgrade, evidence collection across sessions/committed artifacts, retrospective/benchmark recommendations and prohibition on silent deletion. - Closure path: hygiene produces evidence-backed keep/advisory/obsolete recommendations intended to change recurring hooks/skills/standing context, but destructive adaptation is proposed rather than automatically applied; downstream developer action must complete the return into present capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can perform the prospective analysis, yet CocoPlus intentionally withholds autonomous closure over the recurring harness rules; this is therefore a supplied S4 constructor path rather than A. - Evidence: cocohygiene/hygiene.skill.md explicitly defines a model-upgrade audit of governance rules/harness checks and classifies their future status while requiring evidence and proposing rather than silently applying deletions; AGENTS.md.template directs retrospective/hygiene/model benchmarking before changing recurring rules. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic retrospectives/learning are not credited as S4. The positive mapping is narrowly the external model-upgrade path; C reflects incomplete first-party adaptation closure. - External distinction: the capability/behavior of the upgraded external model relative to the assumptions under which current CocoPlus rules were created. - Future / prospective distinction: whether recurring governance/harness checks will remain necessary, become merely advisory or become obsolete for subsequent sessions/runs. - Adaptation option generated: retain the rule, downgrade its standing, replace/adjust it or propose removal when evidence shows it is obsolete. - Path back into current capability / S3: the recommendation targets recurring hooks/skills/standing context used by current runs, but developer action is still needed to apply the proposed structural change; this incomplete closure is why the state is C.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy function is established at the reviewed project/run recursion beyond developer-authored policies, covenants and safety boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: autonomy covenants, hard stops, run policies, safety rules and human approvals constrain operation, but the evidence does not show a legitimate S5 identity/ultimate-policy conversation with closure separate from those constraints/current-control gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party S5 decision right is established. - Decision owner: none established for a qualifying S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: covenant acceptance, run-policy snapshots, hard safety gates, human gates, policy engine, steering restrictions and persistent project configuration. - Closure path: these controls constrain subsequent operation, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 judgment → returned organizational policy loop is established at the chosen recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither a static covenant/policy nor human approval over ordinary current work is sufficient to establish S5; no autonomous or parent S5 closure is inferred from enforcement. - Evidence: README.md, AGENTS.md.template, forge.skill.md and pre-tool-use.js were inspected for ultimate-policy/identity authority; the evidenced functions are current governance, safety and developer control. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351/assessments/cocoplus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351\">e7e949d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T15:07:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MegaAgent</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent/tree/c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\">c2e45ad &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute a bounded project contribution through a model-driven local task/tool loop and produce shared artifacts, tests, integration or other project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, collaborator messages, current shared artifacts, execution/test results, local TODO/status, tool failures and changing subtask needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local tool actions, artifact changes, communication, testing/execution steps and whether to recruit subordinate capacity within assigned constraints. - Decision owner: each model-driven Agent operational unit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent queue/thread, memory and relevant-history retrieval, generated role prompt, bounded model rounds, task/TODO files, first-party tools, shared workspace and external model transport. - Closure path: assigned/local task + collaborator/artifact state → agent model decision → tool/message/file action → returned execution/file/peer observation → subsequent model decision → local deliverable/result. - Boundary reachability: root main.py directly instantiates the generated CEO and workers through the first-party Agent class; recursive addagent uses the same runtime. No downstream agent loop has to be supplied to obtain model-driven operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/tool/runtime code transports and enforces actions, but substantive local work, tool selection, content and follow-up are chosen by each running model agent from current evidence. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, llm.py. - Basis: explicit + structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every generated process is automatically a separate viable recursion; S1 credits task-owning operational agent loops at the declared project level.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate dependency/readiness mismatch and conflicting concurrent mutations among distinct parallel agent S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one unit acting before a required upstream artifact is available; two units observing/writing different versions of a shared artifact; stale overwrites and merge conflicts in the common project workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to adjust local timing/work after collaborator readiness feedback and how to reconcile/retry a shared-artifact update after current content/hash or merge-conflict evidence is returned. - Decision owner: the affected model-driven operational agents, distributed across the collaborating S1 units; the Git lock/base-hash/merge machinery detects and enforces collision constraints but does not choose the revised content/work response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated collaborator identities, asynchronous talk, per-agent TODO/status, shared file visibility, readfile current commit hash, writefile lock/base-hash requirement, Git commit/merge and conflict response. - Closure path: inter-agent dependency or shared-file collision risk → peer readiness message and/or version-aware write attempt → current readiness/content/hash/conflict evidence reaches affected agent → model chooses wait/retry/reconcile/change work → subsequent S1 behavior uses the coordinated state. - Boundary reachability: talk, shared-file read/write and Git conflict handling are standard tools generated for every root-runtime agent; the shipped Grace trace demonstrates the dependency-feedback path without downstream wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a lock alone would be deterministic S2 support only. A is credited because concrete dependency/collision evidence is returned into a model-owned next decision: agents choose to wait, notify collaborators, reread or revise the content/work response. - Evidence: agent.py, llm.py, utils.py, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic messaging/delegation is not the basis; the credited witnesses are concrete dependency timing and shared mutable-artifact collision. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running task-owning agents such as implementation, integration and testing workers. - Inter-S1 disturbance: downstream work can begin before upstream deliverables exist, and parallel agents can act on stale/conflicting versions of shared files. - Attenuating coordination relation: peer readiness/blocker exchange plus hash-aware serialized Git mutation with current-state/conflict feedback. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the receiving model agent waits/resumes/reprioritizes or rereads/reconciles/retries against the returned shared state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: each credited mechanism is tied to a concrete cross-unit instability—dependency readiness or concurrent shared-artifact mutation—and changes the next operational behavior that would otherwise interfere.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the project-wide current portfolio of commitments, priorities, readiness, defects and completion/rework on behalf of the whole organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or blocked workers, changing dependency readiness, integration/testing findings, defects requiring reassignment, project-wide quiescence with unfinished work and apparent completion that may still fail whole-project requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current task allocation/reallocation, testing priorities, corrective owner, additional agent capacity and whether the project should terminate or another work cycle should begin. - Decision owner: the model-driven CEO agent (Bob in the shipped default organization). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker talk reports, CEO subordinate map, shared project artifacts, per-agent TODO/status, global running/idle monitor, automatic wake-up of unfinished agents and final all-idle completion signal. - Closure path: current whole-project reports/artifacts/global completion state → CEO current-control judgment → talk/addagent/inspection/testing/reassignment decision → affected S1 unit changes work → new reports/artifacts/tests return into CEO control. - Boundary reachability: root main.py always creates the generated CEO, sends it the project-start allocation instruction, monitors every live agent/TODO, and returns apparent-completion state to that same CEO for terminate-versus-reassign judgment. The bundled Bob trace shows live priority and corrective routing in this standard path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the global monitor deterministically observes/wakes and supplies state, but it does not choose the organizational response. The CEO model chooses who should work on what next, which defect/priorities matter and whether to create another corrective cycle. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, logs/Bob.log, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: initial decomposition alone would not establish S3. The positive mapping rests on the later live worker/test feedback and CEO intervention/reassignment loop plus the whole-organization completion/rework closure. - Whole-system current view: worker progress/blocker/test messages, shared output artifacts and the root monitor&#39;s all-agent idle/TODO state provide the CEO with current project-level distinctions rather than a single-child result only. - Current-control decision scope: current ownership, priorities, testing focus, corrective reassignment, additional staffing and terminate-versus-rework decisions affecting multiple operational units and whole-project completion. - Current-control intervention: Bob redirects Dave after Grace identifies an AI defect, instructs Grace to continue/re-test and can recruit/message workers or restart work after the final completion check. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: talk/reassignment enters the relevant agent queue; corrected artifacts and new tester/integrator reports return through the same first-party organization. - Why this is S3-specific rather than decomposition / routing / static workflow: the credited witness occurs after initial decomposition and changes live commitments in response to current cross-project evidence and defects.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary implementation/integration claims by inspecting and executing produced artifacts, then return discrepancies into corrective project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: latent functional defects or incomplete behavior that are not revealed by ordinary implementer progress/completion messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine from direct artifact/runtime testing whether a produced behavior passes or exposes a concrete defect and publish that finding into project control. - Decision owner: the distinct model-driven testing agent in the generated organization (Grace in the shipped default run); CEO S3 owns the subsequent corrective allocation rather than the tester silently modifying the implementation claim. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tester role assignment, readfile, execpythonfile/interactive execution, test artifacts/reports, talk to CEO/integrator, shared workspace and CEO reassignment channel. - Closure path: implementer/integrator reports and produced artifacts → separate tester reads/executes/tests operational output → tester identifies discrepancy and reports evidence → CEO routes finding to responsible implementation S1 and orders follow-up/re-test → corrected operation is tested/reported again. - Boundary reachability: the standard root organization-generation path can and in the bundled default run does create a distinct tester; its runtime uses the same first-party tools/message channels and the committed logs show the full finding-to-correction handoff. No external evaluator is imported. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the testing judgment and finding are produced by a separate model agent using direct operational evidence. Deterministic execution/file tools expose reality but do not decide whether the observed behavior satisfies the claim. - Evidence: main.py, llm.py, logs/Grace.log, logs/Bob.log. - Basis: observed bundled trace + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic logs and the CEO&#39;s own routine project monitoring are not credited as S3. The positive witness is the separately assigned tester&#39;s direct artifact/runtime evidence and its corrective return. - Claim being audited: implementation/integration units report that delivered modules and assembled behavior are ready or functioning. - Ordinary reporting path: implementers/integrator send progress/readiness messages to collaborators/CEO and update shared deliverables/status. - Complementary access path: a distinct tester reads the actual produced files and executes targeted behavior/tests rather than accepting those status reports. - Independence boundary: Grace is separately generated/assigned as testing work, not the producer of the AI/integration implementation being challenged; her test observations are reported to the CEO rather than being generated by Dave&#39;s own production loop. - Who acts on findings: Grace reports the hard-AI four-in-a-row failure to Bob; Bob sends the finding to Dave for defensive-logic correction, directs Grace to continue testing, and the later organization requests re-testing/integration feedback. - Why this is S3-specific rather than ordinary review / QA / logging / evaluation: the evidence path materially bypasses ordinary implementer claims, directly probes runtime behavior, finds a contradiction and closes that finding into corrective operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop that changes future MegaAgent organizational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runtime reacts strongly to current task complexity, current shared artifacts and current test outcomes, but no evidenced function models changing external/future conditions for later organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for selecting and promoting a future capability adaptation. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamic agent generation, recursive addagent, memory/history retrieval, current-task testing and task-specific reorganization. - Closure path: current task/complexity → current roster/subtask adaptation → current project execution; no prospective environmental distinction → future adaptation options → selected/promoted later capability → present S3 conversation is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents autonomously change current staffing and work in response to present operational variety, but Profile 0.2.3 explicitly separates current task planning/reaction from S4 future/environment intelligence. - Evidence: README.md, agent.py, config.py. - Basis: structural absence after prospective-adaptation review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dynamically generating hundreds of agents demonstrates current variety amplification/scaling, not by itself future-oriented S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the MegaAgent project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the user project goal, CEO name, model/provider choice, maximum rounds/subordinates and additional prompt constrain current operation, but no identity-level S3–S4 tension or ultimate-policy issue enters an authoritative runtime closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config.initialprompt, additionalprompt, user-edited project goal/provider settings, static limits and CEO role generation. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the CEO is an executive/control role for the current project, but executive naming and ordinary project completion authority are not S5. The user supplies initial purpose/configuration before operation; no parent runtime identity escalation path is shown. - Evidence: config.py, main.py, README.md. - Basis: structural absence after identity/policy review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could wrap MegaAgent in human/institutional governance, but that would be a different system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11/assessments/megaagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11\">2022cc4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAGS</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher/tree/9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\">9bb9dfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist research agents perform the operational scientific work: literature review, proposal development, experiments, manuscript production, and peer review/revision artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended scientific questions, external literature, experiment implementation/execution, result interpretation, and manuscript construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next specialist reasoning/tool/file action needed to produce each role&#39;s research outcome within its SOUL/skill contract. - Decision owner: each first-party builtin OpenAGS Agent executing the corresponding specialist folder/role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL and skill loading, tool engine, memory/session/history, filesystem workspaces, upstream/downstream paths, LiteLLM transport, timeouts, and orchestrator lifecycle. - Closure path: observations/tool results return to the specialist Agent loop; the agent updates role artifacts/status and those outputs become available to downstream agents/coordinator. - Boundary reachability: the repository documents a complete first-party builtin Agent path (Agent.loop) used by the research layer; S1 therefore does not depend on Claude Code/Codex/Cursor/Gemini owning the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party agent engine closes model decision → tool execution → observation → next decision inside the supported builtin mode. - Evidence: builtin-agent execution path, folder-as-agent model, default specialist workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: alternate CLI-agent backends are not used to justify S1 ownership; the credited mode is the self-contained builtin OpenAGS agent engine.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated inter-S1 conflict/oscillation is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: upstream/downstream file contracts, fixed first-pass sequence, TASKS/STATUS files, agent dispatch, and shared project artifacts organize work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 interference witness. - Closure path: workflow outputs feed downstream specialists, but that sequencing/dependency path is not sufficient for S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: named multi-agent roles and communication through files are generic organization/hand-off primitives unless tied to a concrete conflict or oscillation between S1 units and an attenuation loop. - Evidence: file-based agent communication, coordinator first-pass sequence. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: workflow dependency management may prevent some practical clashes, but the required S2-specific disturbance/attenuation witness is not established in the reviewed evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current view and decide which specialist work starts, waits, reruns, or is complete. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or failed stages, changing project status, downstream dependency readiness, review-identified weaknesses, and need to redirect current research effort. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next active specialist/task and, after the first pass, choose which research stages must rerun in response to current project evidence. - Decision owner: the root coordinator Agent in Auto Mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: specialist STATUS.md and TASKS.md, workflow/orchestrator process management, timeout/failure handling, fixed initial stage order, project files, and start/wait/all-complete action protocol. - Closure path: coordinator reads current statuses/review findings, emits startagent/wait/allcomplete/needshuman decisions and writes/assigns specialist tasks; the orchestrator launches or withholds subsequent work accordingly. - Boundary reachability: Auto Mode and the root coordinator are part of the default first-party project template and builtin workflow, not an external operator or development-only controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic orchestration executes start/stop/status mechanics, while the coordinator Agent is explicitly instructed to make pipeline decisions and decide what happens next from the whole project state. - Whole-system current view: all specialist status files, project-level memory/key decisions, current stage completion, and review outputs across the research project. - Current-control decision scope: assignment and ordering of current specialist work, whether to wait, whether the project is complete, and which stages to rerun after review. - Evidence: coordinator Auto Mode and role, workflow protocol. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: fixed first-pass sequencing is enforcement/support; S3 credit comes from the coordinator&#39;s evidenced discretionary current-control decisions, especially status-based dispatch and post-review rerun choice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the manuscript/research claims using complementary access to manuscript, experiment results, literature notes, and external citation verification, then return actionable findings into project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, weak methodology/experiments, citation hallucination or misrepresentation, missing baselines/limitations, and paper-level weaknesses not caught by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make peer-review judgments, classify major/minor concerns, score review criteria, issue a verdict, and prescribe a revision roadmap. - Decision owner: the distinct review Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated review SOUL/folder, read access to manuscript/experiment/literature artifacts, web search for citation spot-checking, structured review format, and persisted review-report.md. - Closure path: review findings are written under review/reviews/; after the first pass the coordinator reads those weaknesses and decides which stages need to rerun, returning audit findings into changed subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: the reviewer is a default specialist in the standard project template and a standard first-pass stage; its report is explicitly part of the coordinator&#39;s iteration mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer makes substantive adversarial/audit judgments rather than merely applying a deterministic syntax gate; the coordinator, not the reviewer, owns corrective project-control decisions. - Claim being audited: the manuscript&#39;s scientific claims, citations, experimental support, logic, completeness, and reproducibility. - Ordinary reporting path: manuscript output and specialist status/results flow normally into the project filesystem and coordinator. - Complementary access path: the reviewer reads the full manuscript plus experiment report plus literature review and independently web-searches sampled citations; it is instructed to adversarially probe alternative explanations/failure conditions/missing experiments. - Independence boundary: a separate review role/folder with its own SOUL and artifact is outside the manuscript-producing agent&#39;s ordinary production loop, while retaining direct access to underlying experiment/literature evidence. - Who acts on findings: the root coordinator reads review weaknesses and selects which stages to rerun; affected specialists then perform the corrective work. - Evidence: reviewer SOUL, coordinator iteration mode. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider independence is not claimed; organizational independence comes from the distinct review role, separate artifact, complementary evidence surfaces, and explicit findings-to-control return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense the external scientific landscape through the literature specialist and turn those distinctions into future-facing research proposals/experiment direction consumed by later S1 work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk of pursuing already-solved, poorly grounded, or scientifically irrelevant directions and missing externally established methods/results that should shape the project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and synthesize externally retrieved papers/field distinctions that should inform the project proposal and later research program. - Decision owner: the literature Agent, with downstream proposal/experiment agents autonomously using the returned research intelligence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper-search skills/web access, literature notes/memory, fixed upstream paths, shared project filesystem, and coordinator sequencing. - Closure path: literature agent searches/reads external work and writes literature notes; the proposal agent explicitly reads ../literature/notes/ and memory.md; experiment/manuscript stages consume the proposal/literature outputs, changing subsequent project work. - Boundary reachability: literature is a default first-party specialist stage in the project template, and its artifacts are wired as upstream inputs to subsequent specialists in every supported runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party literature Agent decides what external evidence is relevant and synthesizes it; file plumbing only returns that intelligence to current capability. - External distinction: current prior papers, methods, results, and gaps retrieved from outside the project. - Future / prospective distinction: which field gaps/constraints should shape the proposal and experiments the project has not yet executed. - Adaptation option generated: literature-grounded research/proposal directions and constraints for experiment design. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted literature notes become explicit upstream inputs to proposal and later experiment/manuscript work under coordinator sequencing. - Evidence: folder/agent upstream-downstream architecture, research workflow skill. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project memory and peer review alone are not used to justify S4; the credit rests on external scientific sensing and a closed return path into future project work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the research-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL role definitions, user project brief, templates, skills, provider configuration, and needshuman escalation constrain operation but do not themselves establish ultimate-policy governance. - Closure path: no evidenced identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator manages current work but is not shown redefining the project&#39;s ultimate identity/policy; generic human input/escalation is not automatically parent-governed S5. - Evidence: coordinator SOUL, architecture. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can direct a project and the coordinator can request human input, but neither fact alone establishes S5 without a genuine ultimate-policy issue and authoritative return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/openags.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T20:00:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCursor</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor/tree/b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\">b4528f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: directly interpret, inspect, edit and verify software against a human coding task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code and runtime behavior, incomplete edits, tool errors and feedback from repository/test inspection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose file reads, edits, tool calls, retries, delegated S1 work and final response based on live evidence. - Decision owner: the model-driven coding agent in Agent mode and coding-worker subagents in supported Project/Multitask modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: VS Code tool bridge, file and shell effect adapters, permission/approval policies, session persistence and job display. - Closure path: agent choice → tool execution/permission result → actual file/shell observation → subsequent model decision or answer. - Boundary reachability: the packaged extension directly wires its agent loop and repository tools, no custom embedding host or optional external agent workflow is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the acting model leaves tool wrappers but no task-driven selection/repair; human approves permitted effects but does not choose every next coding action. - Evidence: README.md; loop.ts; index.ts; files.ts; approvalPolicy.ts - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-workspace change collisions and conflicting edits between independently acting delegated code-writing workers in the shipped coordinator/team modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: at least two concurrently active coding children may attempt incompatible modifications or overwrite/revert another worker&#39;s output in their shared checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model selects nonoverlapping assignments, sends corrective messages when a child flags conflict, and revises affected children rather than blindly accepting conflicting edits. - Decision owner: the lead agent/model in opt-in Project/Multitask configurations; child agents report overlaps; deterministic mailbox and a concurrency cap only execute the decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task schema&#39;s &#39;assign separate files&#39; contract, built-in team protocol&#39;s non-overwrite/flag instruction, stable child IDs, collaborator status, message/follow-up controls. - Closure path: child conflict signal or current roster/result → lead&#39;s scope/plan correction → SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent or new separate assignment → children&#39;s subsequent work; separation also happens prospectively when lead initially assigns distinct files. - Boundary reachability: built-in Project/Multitask prompt paths and child/messaging tools are wired in the standard extension, not derived from a user-created orchestration script. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-scope choice and conflict response are discretionary lead-model decisions; the active-child cap and status store do not own that interference-specific decision. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; teams.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; collaborationState.ts - Basis: explicit mode instructions and structural code-wired feedback - Confidence: medium; a supported decision path, not proof that every conflict is detected or prevented - Caveats: children usually share a workspace; conversation worktrees are optional isolation and not automatic per-child reservations. Ordinary Agent mode does not establish this coordinated path on every turn. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model/tool-running coding children started via the Task tool, each with its own history, task and executable edit tools. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overwriting/reverting another child&#39;s files or incompatible edits in the shared workspace, explicitly addressed by the shipped Team protocol and Task tool. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead chooses separate file assignments; built-in team members must adapt/flag cross-member conflicts, while root can steer/interrupt/reassign them. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child reports and roster responses enter the lead turn; message/follow-up deliver revised instructions to named still-running or resumed children. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped instructions target a concrete shared-file interference, not only transporting tasks; lead feedback can alter worker scopes after conflict.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the set of active delegated commitments across current Project/Multitask coding operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple live coding children compete for bounded concurrent slots, priorities and dependencies while task results and interruptions change the feasible current plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the complete conversation collaborator roster/status and choose which current worker gets each unit, is steered, followed up or interrupted; revise running commitments in response to results. - Decision owner: the opt-in Project/Multitask lead model; not the fixed max-four gate, job registry, event stream or human authorization engine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents and stable IDs, activeAgents map, child status/result feedback, SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent/InterruptAgent, root&#39;s intentionally disabled editing tools. - Closure path: whole-session roster/task reports → lead&#39;s portfolio-level assignment/intervention decision → launch/message/interrupt and changed child work → updated statuses/results. - Boundary reachability: the standard Project and Multitask modes explicitly put an LLM lead over all delegated coding tasks with model-facing operational management tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead model leaves a cap and worker status API but not a discretionary choice of how to reallocate current work; the supported lead modes are wider than one routine child tool call. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; collaborationState.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; taskState.ts - Basis: structural and explicit first-party coordinator-mode instructions - Confidence: medium; session-scoped, not organizational or multi-deployment management - Caveats: not attributed to ordinary single-worker Agent mode or separate VS Code sessions. Does not mean that all child steps are autonomously centralized. - Whole-system current view: ListAgents exposes all retained conversation collaborators, live status/result and queued-message counts; background job registry tracks running/completed work. - Current-control decision scope: lead-mode model decides present unit assignment, bounded concurrency slot utilization, correction/interrupt, follow-up and completion of delegated commitments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect the implementing agent&#39;s code claims using a separate read-only code-reviewer context and raw repository/diff evidence, then return findings for control of repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s self-report or ordinary integrated tool-status check may omit real defects, concurrency problems or unsafe edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate reviewer agent evaluates the actual diff and source context for defects; the implementing parent receives and decides follow-up repair or rejection. - Decision owner: the delegated reviewer model for the complementary judgment, acting only when commissioned, and the lead/implementer model for the downstream corrective choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in read-only code-reviewer and team-code-reviewer roles, child independent history, file-reading/search tools, returned report and parent Task result. - Closure path: parent requests review → separate child reads current repository/diff and forms defect-first findings → report returns to parent history → implementing lead can fix, stop or ask for further check. - Boundary reachability: built-in code-reviewer Task type and built-in team-code-reviewer are available in the shipped extension and run through first-party child-model/tool execution; no user-authored reviewer is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer performs an independent direct-evidence judgment; parent runtime reports/forwards but does not supply the reviewer&#39;s verdict. Ordinary VerificationLedger is excluded from S3 credit. - Evidence: teams.ts; schemas.ts; loop.ts; files.ts; verification.ts - Basis: explicit built-in independent reviewer instructions and source-wired child execution - Confidence: medium-high for opt-in separate-context code review, not for default every-turn checks - Caveats: review and corrective response are conditional on the model/parent invoking the supported reviewer; no claim of Organization S3 governance or fully independent remote operator. - Claim being audited: whether implementing-agent code modifications are correct, safe and maintainable. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer tool outputs, final answer and VerificationLedger test-status summary. - Complementary access path: read-only separately instantiated reviewer model with direct code/diff/repository access and explicit defect-first prompt. - Independence boundary: different child model context/history/role and direct artifacts rather than merely parsing the implementer&#39;s natural-language self-report. - Who acts on findings: calling lead/implementer receives findings through Task result and can select corrective work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external-and-prospective organizational capability-adaptation loop is established in the distributed extension. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external documentation, model/tool changes and potential future feature requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S4-specific adaptive decision/feedback owner identified. - Decision owner: model for present coding and documentation retrieval; user or extension settings for selecting models/skills; neither closes future adaptation of the harness organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: docs discovery/indexing, semantic repository retrieval, reusable personas/instructions, task memory and provider choice. - Closure path: information returns to task context, but no first-party future capability strategy is selected and returned to ongoing operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: document discovery and planning for current coding work is not a prospective S4 organizational judgment. - Evidence: docsPlanner.ts; loop.ts; README.md; taskState.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy constitutional decision path with binding return is supplied for the coding-session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose or policy conflicts as distinct from one tool&#39;s allowed/denied action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 decisive authority; ordinary permission review authorizes individual actions. - Decision owner: user/host settings and project operators outside the assessed system-in-focus; no evidenced internal/parent constitutional decision chain. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, read-only modes, tool restrictions, workspace instructions and team role prompts. - Closure path: action approvals return allow/deny to ordinary task execution; no identity-level matter → parent ultimate decision → changed governing operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool policy is a safety constraint, not the identity-policy function; an operator&#39;s wider development organization is not imported into this one-session boundary. - Evidence: approvalPolicy.ts; loop.ts; schemas.ts; README.md; teams.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea/assessments/opencursor.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea\">37c1aac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:05:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skrvidz/agentic-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agentic Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skrvidz/agentic-harness/tree/c67b6cec088d8f0054d9626adad6a22ea87fdbd6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c67b6cec088d8f0054d9626adad6a22ea87fdbd6\">c67b6ce &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-led project implementation via Claude or Codex engineer turns, including tool/repository feedback and task revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing local source and tests, uncertain implementation requirements, reviewer defects, failed commands and subsequent model observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose code edits, test/inspection commands, interpretation of feedback and implementation strategy inside the supplied objective/current task. - Decision owner: Spawned engineer model CLI as explicit task-operating actor, bounded by Harness and its model/tool permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ClaudeRunner/CodexEngineerRunner, persistent state, per-run worktrees, test evidence, max-cycles and reviewer feedback file. - Closure path: Human objective → engineer model turn and code edits → terminal/tool feedback → engine captures outcome → separate review → revised task fed into next engineer turn or final result. - Boundary reachability: agent-harness run and workspace Run create a real engineer subprocess, while --dry-run is explicitly separate and not credited for autonomous operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Engineer model chooses substantive implementation; removing it leaves only runner/orchestration and scripted test fixtures. - Evidence: CLI invocation, subprocess runners, engineer turn. - Basis: structural first-party executable path. - Confidence: High for decision-loop wiring, medium for actual provider behavior without an authenticated run. - Caveats: The harness does not implement provider transport; installed authenticated CLI models are required outside dry-run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No qualifying S2 loop that detects operational-unit interaction-induced disturbance and returns attenuation into independent peer behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel plan tickets may conflict during Git integration, or dependencies may block scheduling; these are prevented/flagged by fixed isolation/dependency checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Static scheduler selects ready DAG nodes; merge conflicts fail/pause rather than a distinct S2 coordinator negotiating or adapting peer commitments. - Decision owner: Model planner authored the initial task graph; deterministic PlanHarness enforces it with fixed width, worktrees and merge checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Validated dependson, ready[:width], bounded asyncio.gather, isolated TicketWorkspace and fail-on-conflict integration. - Closure path: Plan → independent ticket loops → deterministic merge or pause; no observed conflict signal → inter-S1 attenuation decision → peer behavior correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A dependency graph and isolation are generic task sequencing. Model planning is not proven to monitor competing peers and damp induced interference during current execution. - Evidence: DAG scheduler, worktrees/merge. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Model could have chosen safer dependencies ex ante, but a hypothetical conflict-attenuation behavior is not an evidenced first-party closed S2 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Current-goal control of implementation commitments and task direction based on whole-run state and independent evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Code that does not satisfy goal/acceptance tests, unresolved scope decisions, an incomplete increment, and uncertainty whether a wider ticket plan achieved the objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer decides APPROVED with a next task, REVISE with required changes, DONE or NEEDSHUMAN; the plan-level evaluator judges the integrated project against the whole original objective and may pause delivery. - Decision owner: Dedicated reviewer model for the single-run organization; in plan mode, dedicated planner/final-evaluator model for whole-ticket commitment judgment. Harness applies decisions to future task/current status. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed review schema, recorded run state/criteria, feedback file, nextactor transition, bounded cycle budget, SQLite event artifacts and final-plan evaluation. - Closure path: Current project evidence + engineer work → reviewer compares objective/criteria → chooses revision/next increment/completion/human gate → engine adjusts the next S1 task or stops. In DAG mode, integrated ticket results and source evidence → planner&#39;s final verdict → whole-goal completion or pause. - Whole-system current view: reviewerprompt receives the complete current objective/criteria and source-backed diff/status/test evidence; plannerfinalprompt additionally receives every ticket outcome, full plan and integrated-repo evidence. - Current-control decision scope: Approve a sound increment and redirect the next task, require corrective rework, declare current run complete or require human decision; final plan acceptance can be withheld. This is discretionary present-task commitment control, not a deterministic max-cycle or width guard. - Boundary reachability: Every default engineer turn is followed by reviewer control in Harness.runasync; plan mode invokes final model evaluation after all ticket integrations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects the quality/commitment verdict and next operational instruction from evidence; the Python engine merely branches on the verdict to update S1 commitments. Parent human involvement is a pause/answer gate, not claimed as a separate S3(P) mode. - Evidence: review verdict and actions, current-state prompts, final planner control. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party model choice and return path. - Confidence: Medium-high; model verdict rights are explicit though semantic quality is unobserved in live providers. - Caveats: No independent fleet budget arbitration claimed; scoped to goal commitment/current work in one configured harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate evidence-facing review of operational completion claims and test outcomes before continued implementation or acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Engineer can confidently report a completed change that fails acceptance criteria, hides untracked files or contains regressions not captured in its summary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Read-only reviewer supplies structured findings and REVISE/DONE/APPROVED verdict from direct repository/test evidence; engine records it and routes corrections. - Decision owner: Distinct Codex reviewer CLI session (fresh read-only sandbox), separate from edit-capable engineer; the independent final planner evaluates integrated evidence in DAG mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: collectevidence runs real Git diff/status/untracked listing and optional project test command; review prompt calls engineer text untrusted; Codex --sandbox read-only and JSON schema; persisted audit raw artifacts. - Closure path: Engineer&#39;s report → independent evidence capture plus reviewer primary-source inspection → verdict and cited findings → reviewerfeedback.md/currenttask → next engineer turn, or blocked human decision/completion. - Claim being audited: Work delivered in the active turn meets the stated objective and acceptance criteria and can be accepted as done or as a sound increment. - Ordinary reporting path: The engineer summary and external CLI stream, persisted as pendingengineersummary and transcript artifacts. - Complementary access path: Git diff/stat/status and untracked file listing from the actual working tree, configured test-command output and reviewer direct read-only access to the code, plus independent integrated working-tree evidence for final plan review. - Independence boundary: Separate subprocess agent and session; fresh Codex reviewers start under read-only sandbox, cannot directly modify engineer&#39;s work. Captured evidence is provided by harness code, not inferred from engineer text; resumed sessions retain prior policy. - Who acts on findings: Harness.applyreview converts REVISE into the next engineer assignment; NEEDSHUMAN forces a human question; DONE gates completion. Planner can withhold whole-plan completion. - Boundary reachability: Review is mandatory after each successful engineer turn in default Harness, with nextactor=reviewer durable even when the cycle limit is exhausted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent evidence-backed reviewer judgments are not the same as generic logs or unit tests; they determine whether the operation is revised, continued or completed. - Evidence: evidence collection, read-only reviewer runner, review feedback return. - Basis: explicit + structural independent reviewer path. - Confidence: High for separation, evidence path and corrective wiring; no provider-backed audit of actual generated changes performed here. - Caveats: Missing configured test command is marked unverified; evidence bounded/truncated to 40k chars, and a read-only CLI sandbox still relies on vendor client enforcement outside the first-party product.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective organizational adaptation that changes future harness capabilities in response to an external environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future implementation milestones are decomposed from the present task; no separate environmental foresight is continuously collected to change agent organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model planner defines the current goal&#39;s ticket list, but cannot alter the harness&#39;s future capability, governance or strategy; user changes model/settings manually. - Decision owner: Planner for near-term task plan and human for future system configuration; no S4 actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DAG plan/review, stored transcripts and model selection configuration. - Closure path: Goal → model-authored near-term tickets → implementation; no future environment scan → adaptation option → selection → return to future operating capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Time-ordered ticket planning is present operational decomposition, not strategic outside-and-then environmental intelligence. - Evidence: planner prompt, DAG execution. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An outside organization could adopt findings to redesign its tooling, but that return is not wired in the assessed package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No recurring ultimate organizational identity and policy arbitration loop within the product. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe edits, unsupported decisions and risky plan changes are bounded by configured CLI sandbox/limits and human pause. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human sets objective and decides blocked product/scope questions; no first-party actor adjudicates enduring identity or constitutional policy. - Decision owner: Human user for objective and particular decision; editor/CLI sandbox for fixed access bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Human decision callback/CLI, max cycles, explicit noninteractive pause, read-only reviewer setting and worktree constraints. - Closure path: Risk/product question → human decision → next engineer task, or configured hard limit → pause; neither constitutes policy identity dispute → ultimate arbitration → binding organizational-policy change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operational question/approval and fixed sandbox policy are not S5 policy-authority decision rights. - Evidence: human decision handling, README safety boundary. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User&#39;s employer or external host may retain ultimate policy authority outside this local harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/717a8b4606aa4ea034e6108cf2200e696f90de56/assessments/ag/agentic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 717a8b4606aa4ea034e6108cf2200e696f90de56\">717a8b4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:25:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sinkear/agentwork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agentwork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sinkear/agentwork/tree/3aecbef55ac694d64cb77403092aa850279b60a8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3aecbef55ac694d64cb77403092aa850279b60a8\">3aecbef &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: CLI-model execution of assigned goals and delegated sub-goal code/work tasks, with context and tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing repo contents, requirements, test failures, independent reviewer verdicts, integration-base shifts, consulted advice and human rejection reasons. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose substantive work/edits/tool calls and responses in owner/sub-goal runs; local assignee chooses how to repair a rejected or conflicting revision. - Decision owner: External connected CLI model running under first-party agentwork role/identity token as an explicit operational actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Daemon/machine dispatcher, per-run worktree, assigned agent persona, ACP/JSONL/JSON-RPC transport, role-scoped CLI, timeouts/watchdogs, stateful resume and Git worktree snapshot. - Closure path: Human goal → owner run/model plans/directs → assignee model executes local project changes and reports → run terminal/verification signals → feedback/wake/context into successor agent turns → revised task or completion. - Boundary reachability: Documented agentwork connect launches configured agent CLI runtimes on machine dispatch, and the first-party service creates owner/sub-goal runs with explicit role context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The models decide which sub-goals to create, tools to invoke and code changes to make; deterministic state reconciliation and permission checks are support, not task reasoning. - Evidence: README quick start, run prompt assembly, sub-goal creation. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party operational integration. - Confidence: High for implemented role/feedback reachability, medium for live external-model behavior. - Caveats: No authenticated target-domain agent process was launched in this review; internal behaviors of ACP/CLI backends are not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Detect and attenuate conflicts between independently produced sub-goal changes, directing the responsible assignee to rebase/rework rather than silently integrating an incompatible revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different S1 sub-goal units edit against a shared goal branch and create incompatible integration bases or merged changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A failed owner integration marks a specific Change conflicting, invalidates its prior verified state and enqueues the same assignee on the latest goal base; owner later chooses integration of a new ready revision. - Decision owner: First-party constructor-owned service transition and event/goal coordinator establish the S2-specific return, with model owner initiating integration and assignee choosing the concrete repair. No independent agent-owned S2-specific attenuation policy is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-run isolated branches/worktrees, Change and ordered revisions, integratechange, conditional transitions, qualityiteration and idempotent run enqueue. - Closure path: Parallel sub-goal outputs → owner integrates one into shared branch → subsequent integration conflict → service marks conflict and enqueues affected assignee → assignee reworks against newer base → new revision becomes ready → owner retries integration. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent independently assigned sub-goal implementation runs, each with its own assignee/worktree and Change against the same goal branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A previous sub-goal&#39;s integration changes the shared base, making another independently completed change incompatible at merge. - Attenuating coordination relation: Conflict-specific MarkChangeIntegrated(false) returns the disrupted sub-goal to rework instead of accepting stale output; Change revision history preserves the old failure/new base relation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The assignee receives a successor run and prompt text explicitly identifying the prior conflicting Change and requirement to resolve it on the new integration base. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: A concrete disturbance is generated between independently produced S1 changes, then causes a targeted adjustment in the affected peer&#39;s subsequent work; static worktree isolation alone is not the positive witness. - Boundary reachability: The documented sub-goal/change-integration workflow invokes MarkChangeIntegrated on failed Git integration, with the recovery path wired into normal owner/assignee runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The relation is first-party fixed conflict policy (C), while agent workers provide the actual revision and owner chooses when/what to merge; no general independent S2 judgment actor is shown. - Evidence: collaboration conflict contract, conflict transition/rework, assignee conflict wake. - Basis: explicit + structural constructor control with subsequent operational return. - Confidence: High for specific first-party conflict-loop mechanics. - Caveats: C does not assert a free-standing model coordinator that negotiates all possible peer conflicts; ordinary consult/handoff alone are not counted as S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Own and regulate goal-wide current commitments — sub-goal assignment, integration/recovery and handover — with a separate legitimate human intervention mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing incomplete sub-goals, failed verifications, ready/conflicting changes, stalled owner runs, disputed acceptance and human-requested rework. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent owner may choose assignees/sub-goals, request help, transfer ownership and integrate ready changes from a current goal-wide state; parent human may redirect/assign and reject a goal in review, returning binding correction to the owner. - Decision owner: Agent/squad leader owner model in autonomous mode (A) under goal-role authority; human operator acting through review/assignment UI/API in parent mode (P). Coordinator derives attention but does not make these substantive decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal/sub-goal/change/verification SQLite state, role tokens and requireOwnerOf, owner-only CLI, event-driven OwnerAttention, guarded single owner run, human review state and feedback comments. - Closure path: Goal/sub-goal/change status → derived owner attention and current context → owner model allocates or integrates operational commitments → assignee execution and results → persisted state and next owner wake. Parent mode: goal-wide review evidence → human approve/reject/redirect → binding review transition → a rejected goal creates a new owner run with explicit human reason, or approval triggers delivery. - Whole-system current view: The owner can list goal/sub-goals/changes/verification and sees an actionable wake summary; human UI exposes goal progress, review and changed artifacts. This is the whole declared goal, not an unverified cross-domain fleet command center. - Current-control decision scope: Assigning work to specific S1 units, integrating/reworking their results, changing owner accountability, deciding continuation vs accepted delivery and returning human rejection priorities into next operation; distinct from static concurrency caps. - Boundary reachability: README and Collaboration.v2.md prescribe owner-only subgoal/integration/handoff tools; service rejects non-owner use, wakes owner after actionable changes and records human review decisions via ResolveReview. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The connected owner model owns substantive task division and integration decisions in agent-assigned mode. The human&#39;s accepted/rejected review and reassignment are distinct authorized parent interventions; the daemon only enforces transitions.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separately assigned agent verifier checks a sub-goal&#39;s actual output from an independent stable snapshot and can reject work before owner integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An assignee reports completed while produced code/result violates the sub-goal&#39;s declared requirements or quality expectations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Named verifier agent issues structured passed or rejected with evidence through verifysubgoal; rejected verdict prevents Change admission and enqueues another assignee run. - Decision owner: Explicitly configured distinct verifierid model acting in role=verify; platform authenticates verdict to that named verifier and owns deterministic state transition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Detached Git snapshot for verifier, separate verify run identity, no owner role authority, persisted verificationresult, agent-specific verdict tool and assignee rework loop. - Closure path: Assignee completes and source revision is frozen → verification run inspects stable snapshot → verifier model returns verdict/evidence → platform records result → rejected reopens task and enqueues the assignee with rejection summary, passed creates a ready Change for owner. - Claim being audited: The assignee&#39;s produced sub-goal deliverable is complete/valid enough to become an integrable Change. - Ordinary reporting path: Assignee&#39;s run completion, summary, commits and domain machine-check output recorded on the goal and sub-goal. - Complementary access path: Dedicated verifier run in a detached snapshot of the submitted sub-goal branch can inspect real work/files and issue independent verdict/evidence; named verifier&#39;s verifysubgoal call, not assignee self-report, closes the audit. - Independence boundary: Distinct configured verifier agent/worktree/run/role versus assignee, with a stable detached submitted ref. Service verifies verifier identity, status and running verdict window; a single-actor configuration or default machine verification does not demonstrate independent agent audit. The code validates the named verifierid but does not categorically forbid configuring it equal to assignee ID, so independence must be configured. - Who acts on findings: Service immediately transitions rejected sub-goals into new assignee work with qualityiteration increment and rejection summary injected; passed work goes to owner integration. Squad reviewer comments are separately human-advisory and are not the source of this positive claim. - Boundary reachability: The subgoal API supports verifierid, daemon dispatches role=verify on a stable source snapshot and VerifySubGoal commits the verdict plus subsequent correction run. This is a first-party standard optional mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model verifier decides the substantive completion judgment on independent source evidence; routine machine verification/guardrails are supporting checks only. - Evidence: sub-goal creation/verifier ID, verdict authority and rework, detached verifier workspace. - Basis: explicit + structural complementary audit mode. - Confidence: High for first-party configured verifier control, medium for actual independence when an operator does not select distinct agents. - Caveats: Default verifier is machine-command-based and not a positive agent S3 by itself; human review of squad opinions remains the human&#39;s verdict. Role-level sandbox relies on first-party worktree/role implementation and external execution environments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established agent-owned or parent-closed prospective adaptation of the domain/goal organization&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tracker issues, schedules, acceptance failures and historical task results affect current/recurring work, not a decision on future organization design. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human defines domain policy and runtime/agent configuration; a processor can compile natural-language acceptance rules, but has no authority to choose prospective strategic changes in a future environmental model. - Decision owner: Operator for domain acceptance/agent roster; active agents for present work only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy-text compilation to deterministic checks, history, gate statistics, cron/issue triggers and domain reporting. - Closure path: Human policy → checks/gates → operational verification/delivery; schedule → clone current goal. Neither is environmental futures → adaptation options → strategic selection → reconfiguration return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Acceptance-policy compilation and recurring triggers are present control configuration, not prospective intelligence about external future requirements. - Evidence: policy and trigger overview, domain policy configuration. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A human may use historical results to redesign future capabilities outside this bounded first-party loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No declared closed ultimate-purpose/identity arbitration authority beyond human domain acceptance and operational gates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unapproved side effects, assignee role violation, poor task quality and agent handoff loops are bounded by service/human gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human may define domain acceptance, approve/reject a goal or stop a handoff loop; those are goal acceptance/operational continuation decisions rather than enduring identity/purpose arbitration. - Decision owner: Single human operator for policy configuration and review actions; deterministic service for permission checks. No recurring S5 issue adjudicator distinct from S3 control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal ownership authority, role tokens, acceptance-policy freeze, human checkpoint, handoff-loop guard and audit logs. - Closure path: Human config → operational acceptance checks/permissions, or review → goal rejection/approval → next run/delivery; no mission/identity conflict → legitimate ultimate-policy judgment → changed binding constitution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human final approval does not automatically imply VSM S5; even a domain policy string is an execution constraint rather than an ultimate organizational identity dispute loop. - Evidence: domain/goal policy, review resolution, role authority. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Ultimate identity/purpose decisions may exist in the real operator&#39;s institution, outside this open-source single-user software boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1bce7f1a2ff3c599516a40edbd064de106784076/assessments/ag/agentwork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1bce7f1a2ff3c599516a40edbd064de106784076\">1bce7f1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T12:59:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">orch</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent/tree/533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\">533d06b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Generate and repair code in response to scoped user specification and feedback from tests and model calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar source, incomplete interfaces, implementation errors, rejected model outputs and failing frozen tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a task solution/patch from specification, context and feedback, including changed implementation on failure. - Decision owner: Model-backed planner and builder calls in the installed run; deterministic code enforces schema, attempts and test acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worktrees, contextpack, prompt budgets, frozen tests, provider adaptors, retry ladder. - Closure path: Model plan → task/test contracts → model patch → sandbox test → accept or issue failure feedback → reattempt/replan/park. - Boundary reachability: plan and execute are first-party orchestrator operations reachable through supported CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The planner and code model choose substantive task decomposition and edits; the scheduler does not author those solutions. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L715-L885, orchrun.py#L3247-L3477, orchrun.py#L3848-L3905. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for a reachable source-level loop; no provider-run success reproduced. - Caveats: The normal run requires configured model inference and an executable project environment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: Parallel independently model-backed task builders, each operating in its own task-specific worktree against the same shared integration branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A builder&#39;s code or contract can overlap another builder&#39;s changed files, produce a Git conflict, or invalidate a frozen test already passed by another task. - Attenuating coordination relation: Planned file ownership/dependencies, serialized merge lock, affected-tests integration gate and explicit capped rebasing of collided tasks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflict or affected-test failure returns the named task to READY/rebase or retry with updated branch state rather than silently accepting both edits. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The implemented merge-collision-to-task-retry and cross-task test-interference gate regulate actual interaction failures between distinct coding units. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel code-building units in a shared integration branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: File ownership overlap, incompatible dependency interfaces, parallel Git conflicts and tests broken by another worker&#39;s merge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Coordinate dependency/ownership and merge order, reject a conflicting commit and route the affected task back for a refreshed build. - Decision owner: Deterministic DAG scheduler, interface and file gates and integration merge lock; no separate autonomous coordination actor owns the arbitration policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planned dependency edges, serialized integration, affected-test gate, rebase count and changed-worktree tests. - Closure path: Concurrent task outputs → merge collision/affected tests → reject/rebase/retry of specific task → next task execution on updated integration state. - Boundary reachability: execute, ready, merge are standard paths for concurrent task runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision/feedback witness is function-specific and shipped, but the coordinator&#39;s decisive scheduling/merge arbitration is hard-coded; agent-owned S2 would need composition. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2257, orchrun.py#L2420-L2475, orchrun.py#L3848-L3910. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Mere DAG edges would not establish S2 without the identified collision-response/return path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Whole-system current view: The run&#39;s persisted task table, DONE/READY/RUNNING/PARKED statuses, worker occupancy, rate/spend counters and outstanding dependency graph. - Current-control decision scope: Adjust current concurrency and admissions, reorder ready work by dependency criticality, or stop a module with repeated parked tasks; all are currently code-controlled rather than model-owned decisions. - Function: Control the complete current spec-run portfolio&#39;s admissions, throughput, resource limits and stalled tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider rate-limit pressure, too many active model calls, spend caps, blocked dependencies and repeating module failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change current concurrency, dispatch priority and task admissions or park a failed module based on observed whole-run state. - Decision owner: Deterministic scheduler (drain, room, throttle, breaker); models generate tasks but do not autonomously decide whole-system resource intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AIMD limit, token/cache policies, dependency depth sorting, persistent state, failure counts. - Closure path: Ready/in-flight state and failure signals → scheduler resource/priority intervention → changed dispatch and retry conditions for multiple tasks. - Boundary reachability: Standard execution uses drain and applies current maxjobs/maxverifyjobs, rate and spend gates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A whole-run feedback and resource-decision path exists, but its discretionary control policy is fixed in code, leaving agent ownership uncomposed. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2294, orchrun.py#L2451-L2490. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; no autonomous allocator or qualified human parent mode evidenced. - Caveats: Ordinary user-set quotas do not independently establish P parent governance.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: The planner&#39;s frozen test contract correctly expresses the original user requirements, notwithstanding a builder&#39;s failing implementation or allegation of wrong tests. - Ordinary reporting path: The builder&#39;s candidate code and sandbox failure result are reported to the retry ladder and orchestration state. - Complementary access path: A separately prompted Claude reviewer receives original specification, requirement mapping, frozen test sources and explicit dispute, not just the builder&#39;s success report. - Independence boundary: Separate persistent review ID, pinned input hash and validated testscorrect/testswrong verdict; the builder cannot directly rewrite frozen tests. - Who acts on findings: review closes a correct-test dispute or fixtests commits guarded test corrections and re-verifies affected candidate and prior merges. - Function: Independently challenge the frozen test contract against the original user specification when task failures indicate a test dispute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong or over-constraining tests drafted before implementation, misleading failure attribution and builder attempts to relax constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An exception-review model independently returns testscorrect or testswrong, with an explanation and guarded test edits; a wrong-test verdict can authorize corrective test revision. - Decision owner: Separate Claude review invocation using original specification, protected from the builder&#39;s own test-edit decision; deterministic code validates the verdict and safe application. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable review inputs, durable review store, assertion-count no-weakening gate and regression check of already merged tasks. - Closure path: Failed worker/test dispute → independent review → validated finding → corrected frozen tests or rejection → saved candidate reverified / task retried. - Boundary reachability: review and fixtests are first-party retry-ladder operations; their execution is conditional, not an asserted always-on audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer has the substantive adjudication right; the builder cannot directly alter the pre-frozen test contract, while code checks and transports the verdict. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L3010-L3140, orchreview.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the dispute-triggered complementary path. - Caveats: Independence is functional/prompt-and-role separation, not a demonstrated separate provider organization; ordinary final pytest gates alone would not establish A.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - External distinction: Past external project specifications, sandbox/test outcomes and model-builder failures vary between runs; rule candidates are grounded in these encountered environmental distinctions, not only an internal heartbeat. - Future / prospective distinction: The A/B and replay checks compare candidate rules against distinct past specifications to select instructions expected to improve subsequent planning/building runs. - Adaptation option generated: Model-authored, cross-task reusable planner or builder lessons become candidate rules, tested with/without them and potentially activated at later checkpoints. - Path back into current capability / S3: Promoted rules enter the planner or builder prompt layer in a subsequent run, changing the model&#39;s future action repertoire, while harmful rules are retired. - Function: Learn prospective planning/building-rule changes from accumulated cross-run failures and outcomes, and return successful changes into future model prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeating planning defects, weak tests, recurring builder mistakes and the possibility that a proxy for eventual success becomes misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model review/replanning/build attempts formulate generalizable lessons; A/B evidence evaluates whether a candidate helps, with checkpoint promotion and subsequent prompt influence. - Decision owner: Autonomous models propose the adaptive rule content from task observations; deterministic promotion statistics retain activation veto, so the agent does not own final promotion discretion in isolation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rule store, paired trials and replay, proxy-vs-real Spearman trust test, lifecycle candidate → active → retired and prompt layer budget. - Closure path: Outcomes and model lessons from earlier runs → candidate rule → with/without evaluation → promote/retire at checkpoint → altered next-plan/builder instruction. - Boundary reachability: learn, evolve, settleplannerrules and builder rule trials are first-party modes (automatic rule learning disabled in frozen-policy modes). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous models originate strategic adaptation content, not merely execute a preselected fixed retry; promotion is a statistical safeguard rather than a model-authored option. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L1570-L1775, orchrun.py#L3888-L3925, orchrules.py#L1-L125. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium for prospectively reusable operating-policy learning; causal effectiveness not independently reproduced. - Caveats: Feedback comes chiefly from past run/task outcomes, not market sensing. If an evaluator requires a wholly independent outside-environment sensor rather than cross-run experience, S4 should be revisited; the observed loop is durable future capability change, not just current repair.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-system identity or ultimate-policy dispute and legitimate-authority closure is evidenced for a spec-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe workspace edits, mismatched model routes, insufficient budgets and altered test contracts are addressed as operational guardrails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Bind an ultimate policy/identity judgment to later operations; no such accountable agent or legitimate parent governance loop is shipped. - Decision owner: User chooses configuration and optional frozen policy; model reviews resolve tests and task strategy, not organizational identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy store, sandbox boundary, explicit model settings, pinned planning inputs, cancel/park limits and review gate. - Closure path: Configuration and safety gates constrain a run; no S5-level issue/appeal → authoritative decision → returned binding identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Guarded model choices and an immutable execution policy are not themselves an S5 constitutional decision. - Evidence: orchpolicy.py, orchrun.py#L715-L815. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer policy and a human&#39;s optional local run selection are outside this selected recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3/assessments/or/orchestragent-orch.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3\">987160f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:25:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork/tree/e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\">e97d75c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Claude sessions in a workflow transform requests into code, specifications, reviews and other local project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requests, repository state, test failures, tool results and child reports within each session&#39;s bounded task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete implementation/review actions and revise work from the tool, branch and report feedback. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude agent node in the first-party runtime; an operator supplies the initial task and permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph activation, SDK/CLI driver, tool allowlists, worktrees, event store, session budget and schema validation. - Closure path: agent chooses tools and edits, receives tool/result feedback, changes work and returns a structured report to its graph/leader. - Boundary reachability: claude.worker/claude.leader nodes and their tool/report bridge are supported installed node types, exercised structurally by shipped example workflows; no third-party agent&#39;s hidden control architecture is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: actions in a live session remain discretionary even though workflow graph edges, budgets and output transport are deterministic. - Evidence: scheduler built-ins and delegation, delegation port, factory example. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider execution was not performed in this review; operator credentials and the chosen workflow are prerequisites.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate competing write access among parallel S1 sessions in the same run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two concurrent writing agents can attempt overlapping files/folders or shared named resources, introducing overwrites or merge conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agents select resources to claim, release them and respond to a refused claim by choosing another task or negotiating with the holder; the registry deterministically detects overlap. - Decision owner: agent session for claim selection and the corrective work choice; deterministic claim registry for overlap verdict, holder identity, event delivery and automatic release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: advisory claim registry keyed by normalized paths, refusal and release notices, worktree isolation and message channels. - Closure path: a conflicting claim is refused with holder identity; the agent receives that response, can communicate/replan, and may retry after a release notification, changing the subsequent S1 work target. - Boundary reachability: claim/release tools are supplied to writing Claude nodes when a run exposes parallel writers; the scheduler binds the run-scoped claim port. This is a shipped operating mode, not a hypothetical custom integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ork does not choose the agent&#39;s alternative work in response to contention; its registry only determines the fixed overlap rule. Claim discipline is advisory, so not every actual edit is protected. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running writer leader/worker nodes or spawned worker copies with separate tasks and checkouts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping resource/path edits and resultant destructive interference, specifically documented as the reason for claims. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit per-resource claim refusal, release, holder/contender notification and optional peer messaging. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the refused agent sees the holding node and can work elsewhere or ask it; release notice tells the agent to claim again before editing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: resource overlap is checked in executable first-party code, with a conflict-specific refusal/release feedback loop; message passing is only a support channel. - Evidence: claim registry implementation, Claude claim tool, claims and message specification. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: autonomous conflict handling is agent-instructed but not hard-enforced and was not exercised with a provider here; if the claimed function is defined solely as lock acquisition, that deterministic subdecision is not agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: control a multi-child run&#39;s current commitments and exceptions at a configured leader/whole-run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child failure, run budget/concurrency saturation, exhausted revision loops, and conflicting current delivery commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the leader can choose whether and where to delegate/retry in light of child reports; an owner can answer an exhausted-loop exception, while configuration author sets the initial hard limits. - Decision owner: first-party leader/owner decision ports are available; workflow author must compose a genuinely whole-run supervisory objective and authority envelope. The deterministic runner owns enforcement, not the resource-policy judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: slot/budget accounting, maxWorkers cap, statuses, reports, loop counter, human fallback and halt semantics. - Closure path: delegated children return outcomes and costs; the leader can select follow-on work, or answer an exhausted loop to stop/extend/go onward; the scheduler executes the selected transition. - Boundary reachability: leader tools, child reports and owner-question/answer loop are shipped in the scheduler and Claude runtime; their S3-specific whole-run composition depends on the declared workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are real current-intervention primitives, but a generic leader&#39;s task division alone does not establish a default autonomous whole-run S3 owner for arbitrary user workflows. This supports the narrower constructor C, not universal A. - Whole-system current view: available child reports, run status, costs and configured limits; whether they form an actionable whole-system view is workflow-dependent. - Current-control decision scope: child commitments and retry/loop continuation under configured budgets; no assertion that the agent can revise operator-specified global policy. - Evidence: leader/child delegation, loop owner decision, scheduler budget. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no parent-mode suffix from ordinary human gates, pause/stop or local approval; no runtime end-to-end current-control trace tested.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: inspect implementation claims through separately tasked agents that can directly read the produced branch rather than rely only on the builder&#39;s report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects in the builder&#39;s asserted completion, including security, correctness, UX and code-quality issues overlooked by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent readonly reviewer sessions judge the produced work and return pass/changesrequested; the builder then receives review status and revises in a bounded loop. - Decision owner: separate reviewer Claude agents in the first-party software-factory opt-in mode; graph routing enforces returned verdicts, but is not the audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: readonly checkout, fresh reviewer sessions, join statuses, bounded edge maxRounds, agent approver and optional human publication gate. - Closure path: builder branch is inspected by separately running security/UX/code reviewers; a changesrequested result routes back to builder and triggers repair; passing reviews route to the approver. - Boundary reachability: examples/software-factory.ork.yaml is a distributed executable Ork workflow invoking the installed reviewer node types with readonly project access. The path is an opt-in mode, not assumed present in every workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer models decide the audit verdict based on direct artifact access; deterministic joins merely transport outcomes. Their separation is context/process isolation, not institutional independence from the operator or model supplier. - Claim being audited: builder&#39;s assertion that a branch implements the spec correctly and safely. - Ordinary reporting path: builder&#39;s status, summary and artifact/branch report to the workflow. - Complementary access path: security/UX/code agents in readonly workspaces inspect the actual branch and surrounding code, not just the builder&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: different agent sessions, reviewer roles and readonly source access; all are authored within one user-defined workflow, so a malicious author can weaken the separation. - Who acts on findings: builder corrects the branch when any review requests changes; approver examines the passing result before the user-controlled publication step. - Evidence: factory workflow, PR-review workflow, worktree/delegation behavior. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: source-grounded installed example path, not independently executed S3 evaluation or a universal independent-audit service; routine sequential QA in an arbitrary workflow would not automatically qualify.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally oriented prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external demands or future capabilities could require adaptation, but no shipped owner closes that function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an Ork-run S4. - Decision owner: none demonstrated; workflow authors or maintainers may revise YAML and Ork code outside the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: captured feedback, event store, local feedback list/note/dismiss, context compiler and user-authored scripts. - Closure path: no shipped feedback-to-accepted-change/lesson path into future current run capability at this revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: collection and grouping of past errors are not themselves a future-oriented model/options/return loop. - Evidence: feedback spec with explicit partial implementation, feedback guide, roadmap. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user may build a separate adaptation workflow; that is not shipped S4 closure in the declared default system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy adjudication path for a workflow organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity or policy tensions can arise, but Ork&#39;s gates and constraints concern routine task/runtime permissions and delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S5-specific issue, legitimate authority judgment and return-to-operation path. - Decision owner: workflow author/operator defines configuration and controls ordinary approval; those actions are not an evidenced identity-policy process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: YAML settings, tool allowlists, budget caps, askuser, human gates, CLI run controls and local auth. - Closure path: ordinary approvals/stop and permission decisions affect run progression, not an established ultimate-policy matter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user or agent can edit instruction text or choose publication, but no system-specific S5 right is constructed or closed. - Evidence: workflow format, message and decision controls, factory human gate. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the external human/project may have its own legitimate S5; Ork&#39;s default standard distribution does not supply that governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de/assessments/or/ork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de\">06adb32 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:43:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Synapse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse/tree/b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\">b913457 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous local production and checking of a specific delegated project deliverable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous requirements, codebase/tool errors, rendered defects and incomplete subtasks encountered during task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Role model chooses its own next implementation/test tool after observing results. - Decision owner: Role-specific model agent, reached through Synapse-created engine session; human owns separate scope/permission gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode adapter, per-role tool rules, session brief, tool transcript, provider retries, bounded worker runner and written-artifact ledger. - Closure path: Model tool call→real engine execution→tool feedback→continuing role turn→worker report/files, then return to Architect. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Multi mode makes genuine model-backed role sessions rather than invoking a development mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The role&#39;s model has task-sensitive tool/repair choice; Synapse supplies the enclosing execution and evidence channel. - Evidence: OpenCode adapter, runs.js, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high on reachable code path; no independent Windows-provider execution. - Caveats: Actual model quality, installed/packaged engine availability and end-to-end completion were not live-tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Feedback-based regulation of interference or oscillation among operational S1 task agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits, shared project plan corruption and worker cross-role overwrite risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No specific agent-owned inter-worker collision detection→mediation→returned adjustment evidenced. - Decision owner: Deterministic harness serialization and folder rules; Architect decides task sequence as S3 commitment, not evidenced as S2 conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One-active-worker busy guard per project, roleWriteRules, plan ownership, role folders and assignment ladder. - Closure path: Serial admission and static write rules prevent some conflicts but do not inspect actual inter-unit interference and negotiate mutual change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transport, isolation and top-down task allocation alone are insufficient for S2 at this recursion. - Evidence: runs.js#L546, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the reviewed first-party orchestration paths. - Caveats: A model could discuss competing tasks in an individual session, but no reusable S2-specific interference closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Architect reads the project-wide plan.json task statuses, blocked items, confirmed features and per-worker completion reports grounded in runs.jsonl and actual file/tool records. - Current-control decision scope: Architect alone accepts/rejects task completion, alters the whole plan and selects corrective work and next role commitments within approved project scope; a runner&#39;s retry/backoff merely enforces constraints. - Function: Current project-wide commitment, progress and corrective task regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Partial deliverables, faulty worker claims, blocked tasks, failed providers, scope drift and new defects threatening the approved project goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge each reported worker outcome, update the plan, mark done/retry/blocked, reprioritize or delegate the next current task. - Decision owner: Model-driven Architect in Multi mode, constrained by operator-approved features and optional automatic continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan.json as Architect-only authority, actual runs.jsonl, injected post-worker report, task-role assignment, retry fallback, progress/status rail. - Closure path: Worker run evidence→synthetic report→Architect evaluation/update of plan→next delegate or required operator escalation; Keep building enables continuation, while off returns after each judged task. - Boundary reachability: delegate MCP, run ledger, handleTurn, report and wake are connected in shipped Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the Architect leaves recorded runs and deterministic stops but not discretionary whole-project acceptance, replanning or corrective commitment. - Evidence: runs.js#L2741, plans.js#L1270, delegate MCP. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high; project-control closure is code-visible, not a live success claim. - Caveats: An operator can pause or steer a task, but the documented toggles do not independently establish a distinct parent-governed S3 decision mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Implementing worker&#39;s claim that the shipped build is usable, faithful to approved design and free of target regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder sends its own transcript, modified-file list and completion statement to Architect through runs.jsonl and the synthetic result message. - Complementary access path: Separate QC model role uses the delivered application, renders/screenshots UI, diffs approved mock, tests unusual inputs and writes reproducible findings with IDs independently of worker prose. - Independence boundary: QC has its own configurable model/session and role folder; it cannot edit the build and coders cannot write the QC findings folder under Synapse&#39;s first-party role rules. This is role separation, not tamper-proof institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: Architect reads QC output and delegates the numbered fix to a coder, then assigns verification of that exact repro to QC again, up to the three-round cap. - Function: Independent role-level challenge to implementation claims using real build behavior and a returned quality verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker reports that conceal unusable UI, clipping, misleading states, poor validation or regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: QC chooses and executes adversarial/use-oriented checks, records reproducible findings and re-verifies fixes separately from implementing coders. - Decision owner: Dedicated configured qc model/session, distinct from coder role, with its own findings area and read-only stance toward the build. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QC mandate to render/use the built artifact, screenshot/mock diffs, finding IDs and repros, separate folder permissions, bounded three-round QC/fix/verification plan. - Closure path: QC direct inspection→qcfindings/qc-N.md with evidence→Architect receives report and assigns specific fix task→QC re-runs repros and verifies correction. - Boundary reachability: First-party QC role, delegated real engine session, report loop and role-write restrictions are shipped in Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: QC independently determines and reports actual product defects; the Architect, not QC, retains current-control commitment changes. - Evidence: plans.js#L700, plans.js#L1342, runs.js#L1002. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium: independently tasked source-visible QC path, with no performed cross-provider execution or efficacy audit. - Caveats: The product does not establish strong organizational or cryptographic isolation; different role/model configuration and artifact-based repro constitute the limited claimed independence. If QC is never delegated in a particular plan, the optional path is inactive.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Sensing external/future needs and developing options returned to current project capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unanticipated user needs and future features for the product under construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An Inventor model proposes and ranks extra features; operator chooses scope and Architect links confirmed features to tasks. Whether this amounts to an external sensing→adaptation→S3 loop is unresolved. - Decision owner: Inventor generates options and operator accepts features; no proved future-environment evidence trail connecting the two. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Inventor role mandate, optional local search tools, ideas file, confirmed/rejected features.json and Architect planning instructions. - Closure path: Proposed feature→human selection→confirmed feature/task is explicit; source does not establish that the proposal derived from an observed external prospective distinction rather than imagined additions to a current wish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generating project feature ideas is not, alone, a demonstrated VSM S4 process; the active inventor path prevents a defensible categorical absence claim. - Evidence: plans.js#L660, plans.js#L1287, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: bounded uncertainty. - Caveats: Direct evidence of actual external future-condition observation and its governing choice would resolve the state; neither was reproduced at this source pin.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate ultimate-policy or system identity dispute resolution at project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts over project identity, ultimate purpose and governing policy rather than current task scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party identity-level issue→authority decision→returned governing policy path shown. - Decision owner: Human operator chooses product features, permission and continuity toggles; those remain operational/requirement decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Feature selection forms, prompt constraints, role-specific permission rules and operator approval. - Closure path: Scope selection reaches project plan, but no recognized ultimate-policy decision channel governs a dispute about the system&#39;s identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing which mock to build or whether to continue is not an S5 legitimacy mechanism. - Evidence: plans.js#L1287, automation.js, permissions.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External real-world product governance or developer organizational decisions are out of the assessed standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368/assessments/da/darkdvr-synapse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368\">7211b7a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:22:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ArgusAI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai/tree/5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\">5f7aa08 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out security-analysis and general assistant operations through model-selected tools and separate task-performing children. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code/traffic observations, new user materials, scan errors, tool outputs, environment and evidence changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and sequence domain tools, analyze returned evidence and choose next investigation/validation/report action. - Decision owner: Configured native model-bearing ArgusAI assistant, or connected model-bearing child where given an explicit bounded role; external ACP engines are not credited as ArgusAI&#39;s independent cognitive design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model/tool call loop, domain tool catalog, run/stream state, content archives, confirmation broker and scoped child capability projection. - Closure path: User-defined scope → model/tool call on live workspace → task/evidence output → model inspects and continues/terminates → report or next task state. - Boundary reachability: The native model/tool engine and first-party domain registry are wired into normal GUI/CLI operation, and child tool selection is registered in the shipped assistant. An external ACP model is an alternative supported operating mode, not required for the native witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive task actions; deterministic policy, progress guards and run queues enforce limits rather than owning the choice. - Evidence: assistant loop, tool methods, child execution, README runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for model/tool-path existence; provider execution was not replayed here. - Caveats: A standalone scan engine or GUI table does not own S1 without the configured model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Independently executing persistent Team Room members, each with distinct objective, tool projection, history, inbox and ongoing work; temporary subagents also exist but do not communicate directly. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Repeated peer status chatter loops, duplicate or out-of-order targeted requests, interruption of busy specialists, concurrent competition for shared member capacity and missed handoff dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Team member system context requires model discretion to respond only when its role/objective is implicated, to make concrete @role handoffs and otherwise call teamroompass. Durable inboxes deduplicate input references, queue while busy and preserve explicit request ordering; capacity waiters resume as slots release. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A specialist reviews actual targeted peer/leader messages after finishing its current work, chooses to pass, answer with new evidence or issue an exact-role request; that message enters peer inboxes and changes their next work/action choice. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit model-facing anti-loop rule and selective peer-response/handoff control mitigate inter-agent chatter/attention oscillation, while delivery sequencing/dedup solves concrete concurrent-message interference. A generic chat log alone would not establish the agent-owned decision. - Function: Maintain compatible peer coordination without interrupt/reply storms across simultaneous team work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Redundant chat cycles, missed role-specific requests, reentrant live work and shared member-slot interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide when own role must answer an inter-agent request, pass unproductive chatter, or send a bounded dependency/handoff to another specialist. - Decision owner: Model-bearing persistent member agents exercising model-facing Team Room rules; deterministic queue/dedup/capacity checks only supply delivery discipline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate Team Room log/cursors and pending-input queue, selective @role message route, teamroompass, input reference dedup and member concurrency limits. - Closure path: Peer message/redundant chatter or directed dependency → model member evaluates role fit → pass or targeted correction/handoff → queue records/limits the message → affected member consumes it and adjusts work. - Boundary reachability: Persistent Team Rooms, first-party member prompt and member tools are shipped in the standard GUI/assistant mode; they are not optional third-party skills. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decision whether to escalate/pass/answer is model discretion. Serial/held input alone would not support A, so the positive is restricted to the standing-team model-facing selective coordination path. - Evidence: agent role prompt and member queue, Team Room routing and read cursors, architecture/queue contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no empirical measured chat-loop reduction is claimed. - Caveats: Temporary spawnteam has no peer messaging; this S2 A is confined to the separately supported persistent-team mode, not all single-agent sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Whole-system current view: Parent assistant can read the complete Todo tree, current task/job progress, team roster, child notices, evidence links and tool outputs; owner GUI sees team state and can edit/steer running membership and assignments. - Current-control decision scope: Model chooses investigation phases, work assignments, task continuation, relevant members, tool batches and response to child results. Owner separately changes standing-team staffing, objective/role assignments and active scope of current work, with next-turn effect. - Function: Coordinate immediate work commitments and evidence production for a whole user objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialist readiness, task phase dependencies, uncertain evidence and external tool errors, changing user direction, busy child agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model creates/updates whole-goal Todo plan, delegates and redirects workers; parent owner can edit or pause team members, change scope and direct next assigned work. - Decision owner: Main configured model for autonomous management; installation owner via Team GUI/member update for the parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Todo plan, run locks, live UI, child registry, Team Room and group message stores, confirmation/permission gates. - Closure path: Whole-goal evidence and child notices → model changes priorities/delegation → different agent/tool actions; owner observes team, edits member/assignments or sends new instruction → persisted metadata and injected typed event change subsequent child operation. - Boundary reachability: Both assistant model tools (settodos, spawnteam, agent input) and owner GUI team/member edit controls are first-party installed entry points; they return into running agent sessions rather than isolated dashboards. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous model retains current resource choices; the user separately holds a legitimate parent current-control role over the same local team. Hard permissions/queues alone do not count as a second owner. - Evidence: model planning/delegation methods, child control/notifications, owner team/member editing. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high within installed modes; no actual provider-backed orchestration run was executed. - Caveats: User task interjections are not credited alone; the distinct parent mode is the current-control owner GUI plus persistent member state returned to operation.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Distinct independent examination of operational claims via complementary raw reality rather than ordinary task reporting; full closure cannot be established confidently from the reviewed default distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading child report, inaccurate scan/finding, incomplete task claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge a worker/result with raw Case/Artifact/Tool evidence and return a corrective finding to control; whether a governed non-producer audit owner routinely exercises it is unresolved. - Decision owner: Main assistant may inspect material and can instantiate readonly specialists; the built-in reporter role consolidates findings but is not shown to have a mandatory independently sourced veto/repair channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Case/Evidence original references, readonly subagents, result summaries, operator review, tool histories and durable event logs. - Closure path: Child or domain output → main assistant can reread raw evidence and assign further validation → correction to tasks is possible, but a shipped independent complementary inspection owner and repeatable corrective return are not fully proven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source supports model review as ordinary operations and a purpose-composable review role, yet cannot distinguish a true independent S3 audit from a task-specific secondary analysis in all supported modes. - Evidence: evidence/case store, main review path, readonly specialist presets/child roles. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium for real audit capability, low for verified S3-specific ownership. - Caveats: webaudit reporter summarizes other roles and the human inspects findings; neither automatically supplies independent unfiltered ground-truth inspection. No presumed external security review is credited.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Adapt future patrol priorities, scheduled investigation or long-lived operational capability from external change; specific closed adaptation ownership remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed attack surface, new file/log events, future alerts and cross-session findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and enact a prospective change to future monitoring/adaptation, distinct from simply completing current steps or running fixed Cron commands. - Decision owner: A model may call first-party cronupsert and alter future work triggers; an installation owner can also configure schedules. However no clearly evidenced shipped model process compares new environment trends, decides a durable revised future capability, and closes that decision back into later operating configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cron tasks with interval/cron/webhook/file-change triggers, persisted jobs/run records, long-term session memory and task/Case references. - Closure path: External event → scheduled session inspects current artifacts and may update Todos/schedule → later trigger executes; the decisive model&#39;s prospective adaptation criterion and actual feedback-based reconfiguration are not resolved at this pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model-selectable scheduler plus memory offers a credible first-party adaptation candidate, but scheduling fixed repetitions and reusing prior facts alone are not an established S4 owner. - Evidence: model-visible cronupsert catalog, persistent scheduler, scheduler GUI routes, long-term memory. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for future triggers, insufficient for whether they constitute positive S4 adaptation. - Caveats: ? is caused by an identified plausible shipped path, not by a hypothetical future feature; fixed scheduling should not be mistaken for itself owning outside-and-then intelligence.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What a persistent local Team is for, which named specialist roles constitute it, and which role objectives/tool-power boundaries bind members across sessions. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The legitimate self-hosted installation/team owner can create, recharter and edit its standing team and member objectives through first-party GUI/API; no agent-owned constitutional authority to replace that owner&#39;s ultimate charter is established here. - Return-to-operation path: Owner changes standing team purpose or member role/objective/capabilities → persisted team/member record and pending typed metadata update → next member turn receives new role/objective; tool capability projection/confirmation mode enforces limits. - Function: Maintain binding purpose and role identity of the persistent team as a supported parent-governed arrangement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed organizational assignment, durable role constitution and owner-level policy over specialist identity and allowed operating repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authoritatively change a team/member&#39;s stated continuing purpose, role identity and authorization scope for subsequent work, beyond changing a single tool call. - Decision owner: Installation owner in the supported human-operated GUI/team setup mode; model tools can request team changes but no distinct self-authorized autonomous ultimate-policy closure is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted Team metadata, member records, edited role/objective, capability projection and queued metadataupdatepending typed event. - Closure path: Parent decides a standing team/role-purpose revision → updateteammember persists the charter and emits event → member&#39;s future context/inbox incorporates it → subsequent agent work follows updated purpose/policy. - Boundary reachability: Creating and updating persistent teams and members are shipped, first-party GUI/API features, not repository maintainer policy or an external integration. The member worker reads revised metadata on its next safe turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited S5 mode is owner-authored standing team constitution, not the assistant approving its own tools or choosing current task steps; S3-level task steering remains separately recorded. - Evidence: persistent team create/update, member metadata revision and next-turn notice, member role/permission API schema. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for the local standing team identity loop. - Caveats: One-off job objectives, approval toggles and permission flags alone are not S5. This claim is limited to the persistent-team role/purpose constitution, not governance of unrelated ArgusAI installations or external security organizations.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd/assessments/ar/argusai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd\">49cc349 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T22:45:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andreseloysv/Tirith\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tirith</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andreseloysv/Tirith/tree/be358146324a8a9c98e6649938205328160c79cf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision be358146324a8a9c98e6649938205328160c79cf\">be35814 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-operated coding and repository task execution in separately launched worktree lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code changes, failing tools/tests, context limits and incomplete user-specified plan items. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select edits, terminal/repository operations and subsequent task actions based on observed tool results and brief constraints. - Decision owner: Configured coding-agent model operating each spawned lane; manager is another model-mediated operational conversation for on-call tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model CLI adapters, lane brief, isolated worktree, agent log reader, plan report, hooks, tool gates, retry and timeout bounds. - Closure path: Open plan item → manager/launcher starts coding-model CLI → model acts in worktree and observes results → reports done/partial/blocked → reviewer/manager feedback may cause a corrected new turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipping lane run/launch command builds supported first-party agent aliases and reads real provider output; plan gate and dispatcher route results into the active project plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Plan reservation, test gates and Git merge logic cannot choose the substantive code edit; only the model agent does. - Evidence: agent runner, launcher, launch contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for wired agent invocation and returned status. - Caveats: Agent binaries/provider accounts are external prerequisites; this review did not execute a paid live coding session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent duplicate concurrent ownership and overlapping write scopes between independent operational lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two plan workers claim the same item, write the same folder or operate on an incomplete prerequisite, causing stale or conflicting source changes. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple separately launched model-agent lanes in individual worktrees, possibly concurrent on separate tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Same task ID can be claimed twice, and explicit write paths can overlap including ancestor/descendant folders. - Attenuating coordination relation: SQLite claim/attempt-token transaction, dependency check, lease/heartbeat, write-path overlap check and reported claim refusal; centralized merge queue serializes integration separately. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflicting claims block launch, a missing predecessor remains blocked until actually merged, and released/failed reservations become visible to new launcher/manager decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Path-specific atomic exclusivity and stale-attempt fencing address an identified inter-agent resource collision; failure and recovery change which subsequent S1 unit may begin work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine conflict admission and reclaim/release an expired claim after checking worker liveness. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic coordination constructor (SQLite rules and collector recovery), not a model that can change the reservation policy in the operating mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordination.sqlite3, permanent item IDs, explicit declared writes, lease and attempt token, heartbeat, worker inbox with idempotent messages. - Closure path: Candidate lane → transaction checks existing owners, prerequisites and write overlap → accepted lease or refused conflict → manager/launcher receives available work or blockage and revises later starts. - Boundary reachability: Standard launch acquires reservation before creating worktree/spawning agent; hook/report paths check attempt token; collector recovers dead-owner claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Models select task priorities and write declarations but the final conflict verdict, arbitration and lease recovery run deterministically. - Evidence: coordination implementation, coordination contract, merge queue. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reservation conflicts in declared scopes. - Caveats: Legacy items without declared write paths have no additional path reservation; independent raw shell writes remain bounded by other tool/worktree gates, not this S2 path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the current cross-project plan/worktree portfolio, agent assignments, blocked work, integration and escalation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open work without a worker, stalled/rejected reviews, failed stages, WIP congestion, exhausted agent credits and owner-inbox backlog. - Whole-system current view: Manager brief/queue and Tirith commands expose open plan items, active lanes, status, review/merge queue and recent reports; operator dashboard shows all projects, agents, pipeline results and current owner actions. - Current-control decision scope: Manager chooses split, launch, combine, wait, retry, drop, or ask operator about a current item and handles failed reviews; operator can manually plan-edit, launch/stop agents, switch autopilot and resolve pending current decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base model manager selects current operational commitments across plan items; human parent can directly alter commitments and stop/relaunch under the installed owner UI. - Decision owner: Base A — persistent on-call manager model. Parent P — human owner acting through Tirith desktop/inbox/plan UI; collector enforces allowed decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Resumable manager session, supervisor queue, autopilot proposals, plan gate, lane launcher, merge WIP limits and auto-repair, user inbox and owner-only actions. - Closure path: Base: collector finds ready/failed work → manager reads its queue/plan → decides agent assignment or follow-up → launched lanes and later results change board/plan and manager&#39;s next turn. Parent: owner sees status/alerts → changes plan, runs/stops lane or toggles autopilot → dispatcher/manager adopt changed current work state. - Boundary reachability: Managerd and dispatch are wired into every collector pass; owner inbox, Tauri commands and plan edits are first-party, directly updating the same live plan/dispatch state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model decides which issue to start, combine or defer based on the live portfolio; deterministic plan/merge gates and load limits enforce but do not choose those priorities. Human current-control is an independent supported mode. - Evidence: manager rules/session, autopilot handoff, inbox contract, mergeq. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped control paths. - Caveats: A manager decision may fail or wait despite the tools. Optional CEO audit influences management but does not replace the base task manager with a guaranteed autonomous global governor.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently check the manager&#39;s account of progress/efficiency against raw collector operational measurements and send corrective directives. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A manager can inaccurately claim progress while no lanes run, reviews are stuck, costs grow or main CI remains red; ordinary manager conversation does not expose all such evidence. - Claim being audited: Whether the manager reliably turns the plan into verified mainline work within the operator&#39;s targets. - Ordinary reporting path: Manager&#39;s own chat, inbox answers, plan/queue decisions and per-lane status reports. - Complementary access path: An opt-in separate fresh CEO model lane gets collector-computed merged/started/review-health counts, scoreboards, age of pending requests and operational CI data; it does not read manager&#39;s private memory/full transcript or accept reports as ground truth. - Independence boundary: CEO has a fresh separate model session, read-only source environment and bounded raw-data snapshot independent of the manager&#39;s claimed status; both run under the same host/provider configuration and the operator controls targets. - Who acts on findings: CEO model judges findings and sends checkable directives to the manager inbox; for budget/code/target proposals it asks the human, who decides. Next CEO runs recheck whether directives improved the measured numbers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Make its own evidence-based operational audit judgment, issue corrective policy-level manager directives and test subsequent efficacy from independent raw counters. - Decision owner: Separate model-driven CEO reviewer in an enabled first-party mode; deterministic claimcheck and stages remain support for worker validation rather than this audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional CEO schedule/force-run entry point, fresh read-only lane, collector measurement snapshot, untrusted-data delimiters, typed result validation, findings/directives records and manager inbox. - Closure path: Measured operational discrepancy → CEO judgment from fresh snapshot → model directive/proposal delivered → manager changes current handling or owner resolves proposal → next CEO pass checks effects and reopens/escalates findings. - Boundary reachability: dispatch.run calls ceo.run on every cycle with [ceo] enabled; ceo.start launches a configured model review lane, and ceo.deliver writes actual manager directives/owner proposals. This opt-in path is shipped, not the planned watchdog. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Snapshot and typed parser do not decide which finding matters or which directive to issue; the distinct CEO model owns that judgment, while worker claimcheck and ordinary tests are deterministic checks. - Evidence: CEO implementation, dispatcher wiring, CEO contract, ordinary claim checker. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party independently wired audit mode; no live CEO model output was reproduced. - Caveats: CEO is off by default. Its code-based snapshot does not prove factual claims outside its observed metrics; some planned CEO watchdog/ratings functions are not shipped.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party loop develops environmental/future options and returns a strategic capability adaptation into active portfolio operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model or tooling changes, future project opportunities and emerging external constraints might change strategy, but regular manager and CEO mostly inspect current/past project performance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare prospective external options, select a change to organizational capability and return it to current S3; no such complete chain is established. - Decision owner: Operator/developer chooses provider/model/target configuration; manager decides present plan items and CEO inspects operational KPIs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model fallback chain, Meta summaries, research-capable lanes, plan refresh, CEO targets and autopilot proposal queue. - Closure path: CEO may propose an internal code or budget change and owner may create a task, but there is no required external-environment future-option model or adaptive strategic S4-to-S3 control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Internal throughput monitoring, recovery, scheduling and task planning are not outside-and-then intelligence even if a manager/CEO calls them policy. - Evidence: manager decisions, CEO targets, autopilot. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded absent future function. - Caveats: A user can assign prospective market research work, but that is still a requested S1 task without evidenced strategic S4 return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separately closed first-party constitutional mission/ultimate-policy dilemma resolution at the project portfolio recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Human-only credentials/payment choices, agent tool safety, target budgets, review and merge policy; these are operational risk/priority bounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a conflict about enduring mission/ethos or ultimate authority between current operations and adaptation, and govern later operations through the decision. - Decision owner: Human owner handles personal/financial decisions and sets target values; CEO can recommend targets and manager execution policy, but not replace owner&#39;s ultimate authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner gate/inbox, CEO proposals/approve-decline record, fixed plan/tool/merge hooks, config.toml and planned rules. - Closure path: A CEO recommendation can become an owner approval and plan item for code/budget/targets; this closes scoped operational improvement/authorization, not a demonstrated identity-policy controversy and constitutional return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard rules and a human-final decision are not automatically S5; the cited proposal classes are about task, spend and productivity rather than ultimate identity of the system. - Evidence: owner gate, CEO proposal/decision lifecycle, inbox contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; this is bounded absence, not denial of owner&#39;s real-world governance power. - Caveats: External maintainers and project owners may adopt lasting identity policies beyond the shipped operating workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/75421e2aa581887f73a20a8dc5dc598fe23e4738/assessments/ti/tirith.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 75421e2aa581887f73a20a8dc5dc598fe23e4738\">75421e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
